00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.500 align:middle line:84%
There is a GradeScope
site for this.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.290 align:middle line:84%
Any other questions
before we start?

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.180 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.660 align:middle line:84%
So just a quick
recap, so last time

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.970 align:middle line:84%
we talked about the
Kilian-Micali protocol.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.680 align:middle line:90%
It's an interactive argument.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.550 align:middle line:84%
It's a succinct,
interactive argument

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:42.950 align:middle line:84%
which you can use to prove
membership in any NP language.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.140 align:middle line:84%
And if you recall,
the idea was we

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.930 align:middle line:84%
started before that was kind of
our entrance to cryptography.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.800 align:middle line:84%
So we said the idea of
the Kilian-Micali protocol

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.460 align:middle line:84%
is that you take any PCP,
which is very, very large

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.600 align:middle line:84%
but very efficiently, if you
just have RAM access to it,

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.580 align:middle line:84%
you can verify it
very efficiently.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:03.330 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is you take
this very, very large PCP

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:11.310 align:middle line:84%
and you shrink it to digest
it down using cryptography

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.240 align:middle line:90%
to this kind of succinct digest.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:16.100 align:middle line:84%
This is done using
hash function but,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.300 align:middle line:84%
in particular,
Merkle hash or hash

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.060 align:middle line:84%
function that are succinct
but can be opened locally.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.540 align:middle line:84%
So I'll recall what
this is in a second.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:30.460 align:middle line:84%
And once the prover sends over
a squished form of the PCP,

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:33.580 align:middle line:84%
then the verifier now behaves
like he's a PCP verifier.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.810 align:middle line:84%
He tells the prover, open
this PCP and the location

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.420 align:middle line:84%
that the PCP verifier
would want to open.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.730 align:middle line:90%
And he gets back the openings.

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:48.460 align:middle line:84%
So that's what the
Kilian-Micali protocol is.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.390 align:middle line:90%
But in order to--

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.030 align:middle line:84%
when I said it squishes
down and then there's

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.970 align:middle line:84%
opening, really,
what we need is, A,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.900 align:middle line:90%
we want to take a hash function.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.680 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember,
a hash function is--

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.320 align:middle line:84%
consists of a key
generation algorithm

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.440 align:middle line:84%
that generates a hash key
and an eval algorithm that

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.230 align:middle line:84%
take a hash key and an input
and generates a digest,

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.960 align:middle line:90%
or a hash value.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:19.270 align:middle line:84%
Last time, we talked we--
showed how to get a hash value--

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.760 align:middle line:84%
how to construct from
the discrete log--

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.470 align:middle line:84%
we showed how to
construct a hash function

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.600 align:middle line:90%
from 2 lambda, 2 lambda.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.450 align:middle line:90%
It takes two lambda bits.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:33.600 align:middle line:84%
The lambda bits actually
was z, p to the 2 lambda

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.560 align:middle line:90%
to zp to lambda.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.620 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, one can encode it to
have to be 0, 1 to the 2 lambda

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.540 align:middle line:90%
to 0, 1 to the lambda.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.750 align:middle line:84%
But this is actually not enough
for the Kilian-Micali protocol.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.830 align:middle line:84%
What we actually need is
a collision-resistant hash

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:50.380 align:middle line:90%
function.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.338 align:middle line:84%
First, it needs to be
much bigger because we're

00:03:52.338 --> 00:03:53.680 align:middle line:90%
taking a gigantic PCP.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.610 align:middle line:84%
We want to squish it
way more than half.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.070 align:middle line:90%
This takes-- make--

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:02.530 align:middle line:84%
squishes the size-- reduces the
size from 2 lambda to lambda.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.660 align:middle line:84%
So it squishes it
by a factor of 2.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.706 align:middle line:84%
We actually want to squish
it a much bigger factor.

00:04:07.706 --> 00:04:10.330 align:middle line:90%
So we want to take eval--

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.690 align:middle line:84%
what we use actually
is an eval algorithm

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:16.540 align:middle line:84%
that takes strings of size
2 to the lambda to lambda.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.490 align:middle line:84%
So note here the
lambda is upstairs.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:20.410 align:middle line:84%
It's very different
than 2 lambda.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.820 align:middle line:90%
It's exponential difference.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.270 align:middle line:84%
So we take a hash
function that goes--

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.340 align:middle line:84%
takes gigantic
strings to lambda.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.940 align:middle line:84%
And moreover, another
property that we want

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.480 align:middle line:90%
is we can open locally.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.900 align:middle line:84%
Remember, we said the way
the Kilian-Micali protocol

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:42.570 align:middle line:84%
works is that the verifier asks,
you committed a gigantic PCP.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.870 align:middle line:84%
Open it in location
3, 7, and 15.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:48.695 align:middle line:90%
Now, how do you open it?

00:04:48.695 --> 00:04:52.630 align:middle line:84%
So we also want a
opening algorithm.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:55.340 align:middle line:84%
So really, what we needed when
we talked about Merkle hash,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:58.610 align:middle line:90%
or hashes with local opening--

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:02.180 align:middle line:84%
in addition to having a key
generation and eval algorithm,

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.390 align:middle line:84%
we also have a
open algorithm that

00:05:04.390 --> 00:05:08.607 align:middle line:84%
generates a succinct
opening and which

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:10.690 align:middle line:84%
can be verified in the
verification algorithm that

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:11.630 align:middle line:90%
verifies it.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:14.410 align:middle line:84%
So last time, when we
constructed this Merkle hash--

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.010 align:middle line:90%
I'll recall it today quickly.

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:20.150 align:middle line:84%
And we ended with how-- proving
that it's collision-resistant.

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.340 align:middle line:84%
So that's where we're
going to start today.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:26.080 align:middle line:84%
We're going to just wrap up
the Kilian-Micali protocol

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.930 align:middle line:84%
by explaining the
thing that was left

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:33.400 align:middle line:84%
is to argue that this Merkle
hash construction is, indeed,

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:34.610 align:middle line:90%
collision-resistant.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.290 align:middle line:84%
So that's where we're
going to start today.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:41.950 align:middle line:84%
So once we do that, once we
argue that we have this Merkle

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.970 align:middle line:84%
hash, we prove that it's
collision-resistant,

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.130 align:middle line:84%
then we're done with
the Kilian-Micali.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:51.380 align:middle line:84%
You actually proved that it's
sound under this assumption.

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.130 align:middle line:84%
So where are we heading today
after we finish the Merkle hash?

00:05:55.130 --> 00:06:01.300 align:middle line:84%
Now we actually showed a
succinct argument for all of np.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:02.180 align:middle line:90%
This is great.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:03.790 align:middle line:90%
However, it's interactive.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:07.930 align:middle line:84%
And interactive proofs
or arguments are really--

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.400 align:middle line:90%
they're not very useful.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.120 align:middle line:84%
And the reason they're not very
useful is for many reasons.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:15.290 align:middle line:90%
A, there's latency.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.120 align:middle line:84%
We need to talk back,
forth, back, forth.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.860 align:middle line:84%
But another reason, perhaps
much more important,

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.570 align:middle line:90%
is that it's per verifier.

00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:27.440 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to just prove
to the world some statement,

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:28.760 align:middle line:90%
I can prove it using--

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.120 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to go to
each person in the world

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.200 align:middle line:84%
and give them an
interactive proof.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.060 align:middle line:84%
So it's really
verifier-specific.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.070 align:middle line:84%
Interactive proofs
are verifier-specific.

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:41.420 align:middle line:84%
It convinces the specific
verifier you talk with.

00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:45.540 align:middle line:84%
And we want a prove
that convinces everyone.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:49.340 align:middle line:84%
So that's where-- the
segue we're taking today.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:52.690 align:middle line:84%
How do we construct,
actually, non-interactive,

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:56.470 align:middle line:84%
one that will-- to
reduce interaction?

00:06:56.470 --> 00:07:01.400 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going to show is
how to eliminate interaction.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:02.860 align:middle line:84%
And this is going
to be done using

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.360 align:middle line:84%
a really beautiful paradigm
called the Fiat-Shamir paradigm.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.070 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
explain what it is.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:08.530 align:middle line:90%
It's very, very simple.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:10.900 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to
introduce the random oracle

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:14.710 align:middle line:84%
model, which is a model that
was invented in order to--

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:18.340 align:middle line:84%
an attempt to analyze or
to understand the security

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.130 align:middle line:90%
of this Fiat-Shamir paradigm.

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:23.080 align:middle line:90%
It's an ideal model.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:25.420 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
prove the soundness

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:28.550 align:middle line:84%
of the Kilian-Micali protocol
that we saw last time.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:31.300 align:middle line:84%
We're going to actually
show that it's sound,

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.277 align:middle line:84%
even if you apply the
Fiat-Shamir paradigm to it, as

00:07:34.277 --> 00:07:35.860 align:middle line:84%
long as you rely on
this random oracle

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.505 align:middle line:90%
model and this ideal model.

00:07:38.505 --> 00:07:40.630 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to--
probably this we're going

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:42.165 align:middle line:90%
to only get till next time.

00:07:42.165 --> 00:07:43.540 align:middle line:84%
But we're going
to try to-- we're

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:46.270 align:middle line:84%
going to talk about the
soundness of the Fiat-Shamir

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.200 align:middle line:90%
and the standard model.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.750 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're going to talk
about eliminating interaction

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:53.548 align:middle line:84%
using Fiat-Shamir, talk about
the random oracle model,

00:07:53.548 --> 00:07:56.090 align:middle line:84%
show that, actually, the random
oracle model of this paradigm

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:57.090 align:middle line:90%
works well.

00:07:57.090 --> 00:08:00.690 align:middle line:84%
Often, we'll see exactly when
it works well, when it doesn't.

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:02.660 align:middle line:84%
And then we're
going to talk about,

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:05.967 align:middle line:84%
if we have time maybe--
start talking about security

00:08:05.967 --> 00:08:08.300 align:middle line:84%
in the standard model because
the random oracle model is

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.140 align:middle line:90%
not a-- it's an ideal model.

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:11.220 align:middle line:90%
So is it secure?

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.500 align:middle line:84%
Is it sound in-- when
used in actual reality?

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:16.530 align:middle line:90%
That's the plan.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:17.030 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:17.730 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.938 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You shouldn't be
able to do 2 to the lambda

00:08:19.938 --> 00:08:21.900 align:middle line:90%
because your thing--

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:26.130 align:middle line:84%
if you're hashing something that
long, you'll find a collision?

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:31.430 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: So of course,
you can find a collision.

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:34.159 align:middle line:90%
So we define it--

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:38.690 align:middle line:84%
so the question was about
this 2 to the lambda.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:40.492 align:middle line:90%
We'll see the construction.

00:08:40.492 --> 00:08:41.034 align:middle line:90%
I'll explain.

00:08:41.034 --> 00:08:44.410 align:middle line:90%


00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:46.890 align:middle line:84%
We get completeness
for 2 to the lambda.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:48.950 align:middle line:84%
Now you're talking
about soundness.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:55.490 align:middle line:84%
Soundness says that any poly
adversary or t-time adversary--

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:59.810 align:middle line:84%
poly lambda adversary or t--
poly and t lambda adversary

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:01.500 align:middle line:90%
cannot find a collision.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.062 align:middle line:84%
Now, if this t is
2 to the lambda,

00:09:04.062 --> 00:09:05.520 align:middle line:84%
then you're not
going to be secure.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.390 align:middle line:84%
So the assumption will--
so there's-- this is

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:09.380 align:middle line:90%
for completeness.

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:12.450 align:middle line:84%
For soundness, the assumption
is-- for collision-resistant,

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:17.630 align:middle line:84%
the definition is that no poly
lambda time or poly t lambda,

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:20.210 align:middle line:84%
depending on if you want
standard collision-resistant

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:23.840 align:middle line:84%
or t-collision-resistant,
which is a slightly stronger

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.570 align:middle line:84%
assumption, cannot
find collisions.

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:27.290 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:28.290 align:middle line:90%
Thanks for the question.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:31.940 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:35.780 align:middle line:84%
I like questions-- just
reminding you guys.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:36.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:37.902 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So during
this whole thing,

00:09:37.902 --> 00:09:39.960 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to imagine how
big are the parameters.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.660 align:middle line:84%
So n inside of the input
is something that's--

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.822 align:middle line:84%
normal people can
read, or [INAUDIBLE]?

00:09:47.822 --> 00:09:48.780 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Exactly.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:51.140 align:middle line:84%
So let me talk a little
bit about the parameters.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:52.140 align:middle line:90%
That's a great question.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:54.180 align:middle line:84%
That's a good thing
to start with.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.100 align:middle line:90%
So we want succinctness.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:59.030 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we say succinct,
really, what we want is--

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:01.140 align:middle line:84%
so we get some things
by using cryptography.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.370 align:middle line:84%
Once we use cryptography,
things grow with the security

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:05.730 align:middle line:90%
of the-- that we want.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:08.550 align:middle line:84%
So things will grow with lambda,
which is the security parameter.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.270 align:middle line:84%
What the security parameter
is depends how paranoid

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:12.180 align:middle line:90%
we are in the world.

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:14.790 align:middle line:84%
You can think about
it as 256 bits.

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:17.000 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, it's more,
depending on also

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:18.180 align:middle line:90%
which cryptosystems you use.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.990 align:middle line:84%
But that's a good-- it's
independent of the input.

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:22.950 align:middle line:84%
You want as succinct
as you can get.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:24.740 align:middle line:84%
And if you want to
use cryptography,

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.990 align:middle line:90%
it's like security parameter.

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.480 align:middle line:84%
Now, the input-- it
should-- in some sense,

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.110 align:middle line:84%
it should not
depend on the input.

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.710 align:middle line:84%
But of course, the input is
not going to be more than 2

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:36.560 align:middle line:84%
to the lambda because
you think of lambda

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:41.120 align:middle line:84%
is such that 2 to the lambda
is more than the number

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.780 align:middle line:90%
of molecules in the universe.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:45.510 align:middle line:84%
So nothing is that
big in our world.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:46.820 align:middle line:90%
So you think of the--

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:48.510 align:middle line:90%
x can be bigger.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.420 align:middle line:84%
Actually, the input
can also be small.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:52.712 align:middle line:84%
But then it's-- sometimes,
you don't need cryptography.

00:10:52.712 --> 00:10:54.890 align:middle line:84%
It's so tiny, it's like
cryptography is useless.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:58.100 align:middle line:90%
You can just give the witness.

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:00.950 align:middle line:84%
So usually, we think in
our head of the input

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.700 align:middle line:84%
as being-- can be much
bigger than lambda.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.220 align:middle line:84%
It can be poly
lambda and maybe even

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.180 align:middle line:90%
be lambda to the log lambda.

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.100 align:middle line:84%
But it's not as big
as 2 to the lambda.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:11.840 align:middle line:90%
That's-- doesn't exist.

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:17.330 align:middle line:84%
But it can be anything that's
real-world number, in the sense.

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:21.820 align:middle line:84%
And so that's what
we should think of.

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:25.350 align:middle line:90%


00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So when we
do something like this,

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:31.190 align:middle line:84%
we're trying to reduce the
amount of-- or the size

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:34.910 align:middle line:84%
of the proof from still a
reasonable polynomial size

00:11:34.910 --> 00:11:37.342 align:middle line:84%
thing, but just to
a 256 [INAUDIBLE]?

00:11:37.342 --> 00:11:38.300 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Exactly.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:39.360 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.040 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of it-- you're
trying to-- when you do--

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.420 align:middle line:84%
and let me just repeat
it also for the mic.

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:49.350 align:middle line:84%
When you're trying-- when you
do this interactive argument,

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:53.050 align:middle line:84%
you think of-- you take an
x that's still real-world.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:54.760 align:middle line:84%
You can think of it
polynomial lambda.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:55.567 align:middle line:90%
But it can be more.

00:11:55.567 --> 00:11:57.150 align:middle line:84%
But as long as it's
a real-world thing

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.150 align:middle line:84%
and you reduce it
to 256 bits, that's

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:04.630 align:middle line:84%
the practical idea
you should think of.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:06.600 align:middle line:84%
And the more theoretical
idea is to say,

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:08.490 align:middle line:84%
you can take any input
x, but not more than 2

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:10.440 align:middle line:84%
to the lambda because
that's [RASPBERRY].

00:12:10.440 --> 00:12:12.660 align:middle line:84%
But something--
anything less, and we

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.680 align:middle line:90%
make it as small as poly lambda.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:17.410 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:19.530 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:20.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And to verify
that rho is the opening--

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:23.758 align:middle line:90%
is that what it is?

00:12:23.758 --> 00:12:24.550 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Rho?

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:25.960 align:middle line:90%
Yes, yes, yes.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:27.090 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, yes.

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:28.150 align:middle line:90%
I'll go to it now.

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:31.100 align:middle line:90%


00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:31.600 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:33.430 align:middle line:90%
So let me just finish--

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.720 align:middle line:84%
wrap up what we-- where we left
last time, which is the Merkle

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:37.240 align:middle line:90%
hash.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:40.000 align:middle line:84%
So we use Merkle hash in
the Kilian-Micali protocol.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:41.888 align:middle line:84%
Actually, we don't
need to really remember

00:12:41.888 --> 00:12:43.430 align:middle line:84%
the Kilian-Micali
protocol for today.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.410 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to
actually really use it.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.583 align:middle line:84%
But let me still
finish the Merkle hash

00:12:47.583 --> 00:12:49.250 align:middle line:84%
because it's a very
important primitive.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.320 align:middle line:84%
It's used everywhere
in cryptography.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:53.332 align:middle line:90%
So it's very important to know.

00:12:53.332 --> 00:12:55.070 align:middle line:90%
So what is the Merkle hash?

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:58.840 align:middle line:84%
It takes any hash function,
not local opening, just

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.090 align:middle line:84%
a regular hash function
that generates a hash key

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:02.440 align:middle line:90%
and has an eval.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:04.300 align:middle line:84%
Algorithm evaluates,
just generates

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:09.398 align:middle line:84%
hash value, such that it takes
two lambda bits to lambda bits.

00:13:09.398 --> 00:13:10.690 align:middle line:90%
Actually, you can make it more.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:13.420 align:middle line:84%
It can be even 1 plus epsilon
lambda bits to lambda.

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:14.360 align:middle line:90%
You can play with it.

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.740 align:middle line:84%
But let's say two
lambda, two lambda bits.

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:22.760 align:middle line:84%
And we construct from it a hash
function with local opening.

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.150 align:middle line:84%
So let me tell you exactly
what this hash function

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.520 align:middle line:84%
with local opening--
what it looks like.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:31.250 align:middle line:84%
So the gen, the hash key, is
exactly the same as before.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:34.750 align:middle line:84%
We take the same hash key as
our underlying-- so first,

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:36.200 align:middle line:90%
we started with a hash.

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:39.100 align:middle line:84%
We started with
this hash family.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:39.757 align:middle line:90%
What is it?

00:13:39.757 --> 00:13:41.590 align:middle line:84%
Last time, we saw we
can take-- for example,

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:43.400 align:middle line:84%
I gave an example
with the discrete log.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.900 align:middle line:84%
But you can even-- if you don't
remember, it doesn't matter.

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.810 align:middle line:84%
Think of it-- someone
gave you a hash family

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:52.030 align:middle line:90%
with this domain and range.

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:54.520 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm going to do
a domain extension.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:56.190 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to make the
domain much bigger

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:58.150 align:middle line:90%
and get local opening.

00:13:58.150 --> 00:13:59.380 align:middle line:90%
How do I do it?

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:02.580 align:middle line:84%
So this is the Merkle
hash construction.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:05.850 align:middle line:84%
The idea is-- so the
gen is, as I said-- just

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:09.060 align:middle line:84%
generate the same hash key as
the original gen, no change

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:10.420 align:middle line:90%
to the gen algorithm.

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:12.580 align:middle line:84%
The eval algorithm, on
the other hand, of course,

00:14:12.580 --> 00:14:15.540 align:middle line:84%
changes because the
domain is much bigger.

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:18.730 align:middle line:84%
It takes strings of size, at
most, 2 to the lambda bits.

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:20.200 align:middle line:90%
And what does it do?

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:22.150 align:middle line:84%
So what it does
is the following.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:23.560 align:middle line:90%
It takes as input x.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:27.180 align:middle line:84%
Let's suppose, for
simplicity, that x is exactly

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.075 align:middle line:90%
2 to the l times lambda bits.

00:14:30.075 --> 00:14:34.244 align:middle line:90%


00:14:34.244 --> 00:14:35.800 align:middle line:90%
You don't have to assume this.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.240 align:middle line:90%
This is just easy to assume.

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:38.880 align:middle line:84%
And you can always
get it with padding.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:41.180 align:middle line:84%
Take any input and pad it
so it will be of this form.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:42.980 align:middle line:90%
And now hash it.

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:45.550 align:middle line:84%
I'll say a word if
it's not of this form.

00:14:45.550 --> 00:14:48.470 align:middle line:84%
But if it is of this
form, what do you do?

00:14:48.470 --> 00:14:50.770 align:middle line:90%
You take your input x.

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:53.870 align:middle line:84%
Just partition it to blocks,
each of length lambda.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:56.530 align:middle line:90%
So you have 2 to the l blocks.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:15:00.670 align:middle line:84%
You have an input x of length
2 to the l times lambda.

00:15:00.670 --> 00:15:04.240 align:middle line:84%
You partition it to chunks,
each of length lambda.

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:05.960 align:middle line:90%
So you have 2 to the l chunks.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:10.360 align:middle line:84%
Let's call the chunks x1, x2,
x3, x4, and so on, up to x 2

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:12.580 align:middle line:90%
to the lambda, each of them--

00:15:12.580 --> 00:15:17.650 align:middle line:84%
up to x2 to the l-- sorry,
each of them of length lambda.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:19.120 align:middle line:90%
And now what you do--

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:20.510 align:middle line:90%
you take two blocks--

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:21.910 align:middle line:90%
this is two lambda bits--

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:26.350 align:middle line:84%
and hash it using our
building block hash

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:28.190 align:middle line:90%
that takes 2 lambda to lambda.

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:29.720 align:middle line:90%
You take the next two lambda.

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:31.090 align:middle line:90%
You hash.

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:32.360 align:middle line:90%
You take these two.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:33.400 align:middle line:90%
You hash.

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:36.700 align:middle line:84%
So in other words, you
take that 2 to the l

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:41.900 align:middle line:84%
blocks to 2 to the
l minus 1 blocks.

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:42.790 align:middle line:90%
Every pair, you hash.

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:43.580 align:middle line:90%
Every pair, you hash.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:44.230 align:middle line:90%
Every pair, you hash.

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:45.105 align:middle line:90%
Every pair, you hash.

00:15:45.105 --> 00:15:49.100 align:middle line:84%
You got 2 to the
l minus 1 blocks.

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:50.360 align:middle line:90%
Then you take these blocks.

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:51.875 align:middle line:90%
And again, every pair, you hash.

00:15:51.875 --> 00:15:52.750 align:middle line:90%
Every pair, you hash.

00:15:52.750 --> 00:15:55.630 align:middle line:84%
Every pair, you hash 2
to the l minus 2 blocks,

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:59.470 align:middle line:84%
and so on and so forth until you
arrive to one block at the end.

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:00.770 align:middle line:90%
What do you output?

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:02.860 align:middle line:90%
You output the top, the final--

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:05.320 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be
somewhere here--

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:09.130 align:middle line:84%
block, which is
often called a root.

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:10.480 align:middle line:90%
You output the root.

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:13.350 align:middle line:84%
And you output the
depth of the tree.

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:14.600 align:middle line:90%
That's the output of the hash.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:16.100 align:middle line:90%
So the output is the root.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:19.480 align:middle line:84%
The hash value here--
it's lambda bits

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:22.030 align:middle line:84%
and d, which you also think
of it as being lambda bits.

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:25.030 align:middle line:84%
Even if it's less, just
encode it using lambda bits.

00:16:25.030 --> 00:16:27.550 align:middle line:84%
So if you're mad now at
me because I promised you

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:31.210 align:middle line:84%
lambda bits and you see here
two lambda bits, apply this

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:34.660 align:middle line:84%
hash one more time, and
you'll get your lambda bits.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:37.200 align:middle line:84%
But having two lambda
bits is also fine.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.152 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:16:38.152 --> 00:16:41.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why are we bounding
the input length to 2

00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:43.680 align:middle line:84%
to the lambda instead
of just arbitrary?

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:43.833 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Good.

00:16:43.833 --> 00:16:43.871 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:16:43.871 --> 00:16:43.910 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:44.091 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:16:44.091 --> 00:16:44.273 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:16:44.273 --> 00:16:44.922 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:16:44.922 --> 00:16:46.380 align:middle line:84%
Because last time--
great question.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:47.755 align:middle line:84%
So last time, when
I mentioned, I

00:16:47.755 --> 00:16:50.130 align:middle line:84%
said, just take
star here, anything.

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:53.970 align:middle line:84%
So why am I bounding
it out to 2 the lambda?

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:59.190 align:middle line:84%
So this construction bounds 2 to
the lambda because as you see,

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.170 align:middle line:90%
I take every lambda I can take.

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:02.160 align:middle line:90%
That's all it gives me.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:03.330 align:middle line:90%
So that's all I get.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:05.280 align:middle line:84%
Now you can say, wait,
what if I want more?

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:06.599 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I want 2 to the lambda.

00:17:06.599 --> 00:17:08.790 align:middle line:84%
You can actually get more
than 2 to the lambda.

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:10.920 align:middle line:84%
For example, you
can bootstrap this.

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:14.240 align:middle line:84%
So now you can take 2
to the lambda to lambda.

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:18.812 align:middle line:84%
You can create a Merkle
hash of this Merkle hash.

00:17:18.812 --> 00:17:21.410 align:middle line:90%
But actually, you never--

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:23.420 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't come up
because you never need to.

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:25.145 align:middle line:90%
2 to the lambda is--

00:17:25.145 --> 00:17:26.900 align:middle line:84%
think nothing is more
than 2 to the-- we

00:17:26.900 --> 00:17:28.560 align:middle line:84%
choose lambda so
that in our world,

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:30.630 align:middle line:84%
nothing is more than
size 2 to the lambda.

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.161 align:middle line:90%
But you can think of--

00:17:33.161 --> 00:17:36.690 align:middle line:84%
you can get-- if you-- just
as a mathematical curiosity,

00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:40.040 align:middle line:90%
you can apply it.

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.120 align:middle line:84%
So once you have from 2
to the lambda to lambda,

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:46.010 align:middle line:84%
you can chop your things
to 2 to the lambda

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:48.980 align:middle line:84%
and do this-- use
this as a building

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:50.340 align:middle line:90%
block to a bigger Merkle hash.

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:53.540 align:middle line:90%
So you can amplify it.

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:57.510 align:middle line:84%
But we're not-- for us, 2 to the
lambda to lambda is good enough.

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:58.010 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:00.410 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:03.300 align:middle line:90%
Any other question?

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:04.405 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:18:04.405 --> 00:18:05.780 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Why does
the depth need

00:18:05.780 --> 00:18:07.130 align:middle line:90%
to be included in the output?

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:09.447 align:middle line:84%
Should it not just be a
function of the length of x?

00:18:09.447 --> 00:18:10.280 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Good.

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:11.520 align:middle line:90%
Great, great question.

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:14.400 align:middle line:90%
Why do I insist on including d?

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:15.780 align:middle line:90%
Is it not a function of x?

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:16.770 align:middle line:90%
It is a function of x.

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:18.978 align:middle line:84%
It's a deterministic function
of the length, not even

00:18:18.978 --> 00:18:20.280 align:middle line:90%
of x-- of the length of x.

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:20.960 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:23.460 align:middle line:90%
However, nobody knows what x is.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:26.970 align:middle line:84%
So at the end of the day, I
want collision resistance.

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:29.510 align:middle line:84%
And so the property
we want from this hash

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:31.080 align:middle line:84%
is that it's
collision resistance.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:32.700 align:middle line:84%
What does
collision-resistant means?

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:37.430 align:middle line:84%
It means that you cannot output
a hash value with two different

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:38.720 align:middle line:90%
openings.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:41.990 align:middle line:84%
Now, when you open a hash value,
nobody knows what the x is.

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:44.220 align:middle line:84%
Actually, you may
not even know--

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:48.260 align:middle line:84%
well, if you-- when
you open, you know.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:53.180 align:middle line:84%
But when you give someone a hash
value, there's no x inherently--

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:54.330 align:middle line:90%
a part of it.

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:56.937 align:middle line:90%
If you don't include the depth--

00:18:56.937 --> 00:18:59.520 align:middle line:84%
actually, if there's an attack,
it's not collision resistance.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:00.210 align:middle line:90%
It's really not.

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:02.670 align:middle line:84%
It's not just a-- why is it
not collision resistance?

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:06.510 align:middle line:84%
For example, just as an attack,
let's say I publish this root,

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:07.430 align:middle line:90%
even--

00:19:07.430 --> 00:19:08.580 align:middle line:90%
this is my root.

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:09.750 align:middle line:90%
This is my input.

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:12.020 align:middle line:90%
My input is x1, x2, x3, x4.

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:13.740 align:middle line:90%
And the output is root.

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:16.560 align:middle line:84%
Now let me break the
collision resistance.

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:18.210 align:middle line:84%
I'll give you two
different openings.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:20.850 align:middle line:90%
One is x1, x2, x3, x4.

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:21.630 align:middle line:90%
You do the hash?

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:22.650 align:middle line:90%
You see you're happy.

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:23.960 align:middle line:90%
It's a good opening.

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:27.920 align:middle line:84%
Now let me give you another
opening, just this and this.

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:29.630 align:middle line:90%
That's also a good opening.

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:32.330 align:middle line:84%
So I open it in
two different ways.

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:33.480 align:middle line:90%
That's not okay.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:34.880 align:middle line:90%
That's a collision.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:38.390 align:middle line:84%
If you include d,
however, then--

00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:40.570 align:middle line:84%
so that's exactly
what will allow me

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:41.820 align:middle line:90%
to argue collision resistance.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:43.580 align:middle line:90%
It's this d.

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:44.220 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:46.760 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:49.580 align:middle line:90%
So this is the construction.

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:51.630 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:52.130 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:52.950 align:middle line:90%
How do we open?

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.800 align:middle line:84%
So now this is
just so far great.

00:19:55.800 --> 00:19:58.023 align:middle line:84%
We managed to go from 2
to the lambda to lambda.

00:19:58.023 --> 00:19:59.690 align:middle line:84%
I didn't argue collision
resistance yet.

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:02.420 align:middle line:84%
But at least we did
domain extension.

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.020 align:middle line:90%
So we extended the domain.

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:07.130 align:middle line:84%
But I also want-- especially,
I need it for the Kilian-Micali

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:09.960 align:middle line:84%
protocol and for many, many
other uses in cryptography--

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:13.020 align:middle line:84%
I want to be able
to do local opening.

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:15.860 align:middle line:84%
So remember, what does
local opening mean?

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:18.230 align:middle line:90%
I may not want to open when--

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:22.160 align:middle line:84%
with-- I may not want to
give you my entire input.

00:20:22.160 --> 00:20:23.660 align:middle line:90%
I may just want to give you--

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:26.750 align:middle line:84%
to convince you
that this hash value

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:31.930 align:middle line:84%
in some location
i of the input--

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:34.045 align:middle line:84%
the value is a
big B. But I don't

00:20:34.045 --> 00:20:36.420 align:middle line:84%
want to give you the entire--
because the entire input is

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:36.920 align:middle line:90%
gigantic.

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:38.850 align:middle line:90%
You can't even hold it.

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:40.630 align:middle line:90%
So I want a succinct opening.

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:43.870 align:middle line:84%
I want to convince you
in a very succinct way

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:48.510 align:middle line:84%
that what's sitting here
in this bit is 0 or is 1.

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:50.110 align:middle line:90%
So how do I do it?

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:55.900 align:middle line:84%
So this construction is
actually really nice.

00:20:55.900 --> 00:20:58.300 align:middle line:84%
It gives you a local
opening in a very nice way.

00:20:58.300 --> 00:20:59.680 align:middle line:90%
So what's the local opening?

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:01.950 align:middle line:90%
Suppose I want to open this--

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:03.690 align:middle line:90%
the bit here sitting here.

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:05.190 align:middle line:90%
What I'm going to do--

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:07.840 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to actually give
you this entire block.

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:08.800 align:middle line:90%
It's only lambda bits.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:10.493 align:middle line:90%
So it's not that big.

00:21:10.493 --> 00:21:11.410 align:middle line:90%
I give you this block.

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:12.520 align:middle line:90%
What do you do with it?

00:21:12.520 --> 00:21:16.450 align:middle line:84%
So let me help you-- convince
you that this block is, indeed,

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:18.070 align:middle line:90%
the block related to the root.

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:23.200 align:middle line:84%
Namely, it's the block that,
indeed, sits here next to--

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:25.440 align:middle line:90%
how do I convince you?

00:21:25.440 --> 00:21:27.280 align:middle line:90%
I give you the sibling.

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:29.650 align:middle line:90%
So this block has a parent.

00:21:29.650 --> 00:21:32.790 align:middle line:84%
I give you the sibling
and the parent.

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:34.810 align:middle line:90%
This parent has a parent.

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:38.850 align:middle line:84%
I give you the sibling and the
parent, and so on and so forth,

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:40.380 align:middle line:90%
until you get to the root.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:42.340 align:middle line:90%
I give you all these blocks.

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:47.320 align:middle line:84%
So in each layer,
I open two blocks,

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:48.910 align:middle line:90%
which are the two siblings.

00:21:48.910 --> 00:21:50.128 align:middle line:90%
So I open two siblings.

00:21:50.128 --> 00:21:50.670 align:middle line:90%
You get this.

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:51.760 align:middle line:90%
I open the two siblings.

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:52.302 align:middle line:90%
You get this.

00:21:52.302 --> 00:21:53.830 align:middle line:84%
I open the two
siblings, and so on.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:54.720 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:56.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This is
a constant factor.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:57.730 align:middle line:90%
So I don't know if it matters.

00:21:57.730 --> 00:21:59.980 align:middle line:84%
But do you strictly need
to return the ancestors?

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:01.740 align:middle line:84%
You can only return
the siblings, right?

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:01.866 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Good.

00:22:01.866 --> 00:22:01.929 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:22:01.929 --> 00:22:01.995 align:middle line:90%
Good.

00:22:01.995 --> 00:22:02.250 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:02.585 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:22:02.585 --> 00:22:02.920 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:03.795 align:middle line:90%
You're totally right.

00:22:03.795 --> 00:22:05.910 align:middle line:84%
You're saying, well, if
you really want to be--

00:22:05.910 --> 00:22:08.118 align:middle line:84%
what you're saying, which
is completely right thing--

00:22:08.118 --> 00:22:09.880 align:middle line:84%
this is wasteful
because actually,

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:11.680 align:middle line:84%
all I need to give
you is the sibling.

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:14.250 align:middle line:90%
So why waste communication?

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:17.840 align:middle line:84%
If I want to open this, I'll
give you this and this sibling.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:19.090 align:middle line:90%
I don't need to give you this.

00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:20.533 align:middle line:84%
You can compute
this on your own.

00:22:20.533 --> 00:22:21.700 align:middle line:90%
So compute this on your own.

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:22.710 align:middle line:90%
I'll give you this.

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:24.250 align:middle line:90%
I don't need to give you this.

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:25.090 align:middle line:90%
You have these two.

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:26.610 align:middle line:90%
So compute this on your own.

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:29.980 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll only give you
this and then-- and so on.

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:31.300 align:middle line:90%
You're 100% correct.

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:34.110 align:middle line:84%
I actually just need
to give you one.

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:37.170 align:middle line:84%
I wrote two because
it's just easier to say.

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:39.420 align:middle line:90%
But yes, exactly, one is enough.

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:42.660 align:middle line:90%
And you can compute the--

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:45.510 align:middle line:90%
fantastic.

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:47.980 align:middle line:90%
So I denote it by--

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:50.250 align:middle line:90%
so what does the open do?

00:22:50.250 --> 00:22:53.250 align:middle line:84%
For each layer, it
gives you two blocks

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:54.910 align:middle line:90%
for layer j denoted by zj.

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:57.630 align:middle line:90%
So zj is two blocks.

00:22:57.630 --> 00:22:59.640 align:middle line:84%
And the leaves--
it's two blocks.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:00.520 align:middle line:90%
j equals 0.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:02.740 align:middle line:84%
It's in the leaf-- layer
1, layer 2, up to layer d.

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:03.907 align:middle line:90%
It's actually just the root.

00:23:03.907 --> 00:23:07.680 align:middle line:84%
So you can actually go
to d minus 1 if you want.

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:12.750 align:middle line:84%
But I'll just-- in my mind,
I want to include the root.

00:23:12.750 --> 00:23:16.450 align:middle line:84%
The reason I include both in the
root is now when I analyze it,

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:18.880 align:middle line:84%
it's-- notation-wise,
it's just very, very easy.

00:23:18.880 --> 00:23:21.780 align:middle line:84%
But really, it's enough, like
you said, to have the sibling.

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:24.930 align:middle line:84%
And the roots, of course,
you don't need to give.

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.440 align:middle line:84%
But let's just think,
this is how I open.

00:23:27.440 --> 00:23:29.740 align:middle line:90%
And now how do I verify?

00:23:29.740 --> 00:23:33.160 align:middle line:84%
I just take each two
sibling, compute the hash,

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:37.060 align:middle line:84%
and make sure it corresponds
to the relevant z's--

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:38.690 align:middle line:90%
indeed, the parent.

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:40.880 align:middle line:84%
So again, you gave
me all these things.

00:23:40.880 --> 00:23:42.440 align:middle line:90%
I compute these two.

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:44.958 align:middle line:84%
I check that it's consistent
with what you gave you,

00:23:44.958 --> 00:23:46.250 align:middle line:90%
or if you didn't give me, fine.

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:47.690 align:middle line:90%
I'll compute it on my own.

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:48.680 align:middle line:90%
I get this.

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:51.065 align:middle line:90%
I'll compute this and give in.

00:23:51.065 --> 00:23:53.440 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll check that--
all I need to check at the end

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:54.648 align:middle line:90%
is consistency with the root.

00:23:54.648 --> 00:23:58.255 align:middle line:90%


00:23:58.255 --> 00:24:01.870 align:middle line:90%
That's the verification.

00:24:01.870 --> 00:24:05.336 align:middle line:84%
Question about the
design, the algorithms?

00:24:05.336 --> 00:24:08.440 align:middle line:90%


00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:11.120 align:middle line:84%
So this is where we left
off, actually, last time.

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:14.860 align:middle line:84%
And what I want to do now is
do the collision resistance.

00:24:14.860 --> 00:24:16.720 align:middle line:84%
I'm happy that,
actually, we got a chance

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:19.370 align:middle line:84%
to do it again because it's
a very important primitive.

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:23.010 align:middle line:84%
So it's worth having it
ingrained in your mind.

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:25.810 align:middle line:90%


00:24:25.810 --> 00:24:27.980 align:middle line:84%
So why is it
collision-resistant?

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:30.550 align:middle line:90%


00:24:30.550 --> 00:24:32.600 align:middle line:84%
So to prove
collision resistance,

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:33.790 align:middle line:90%
what do we want to argue?

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:39.340 align:middle line:84%
That an adversary that's given
a hash key cannot produce a hash

00:24:39.340 --> 00:24:39.950 align:middle line:90%
value--

00:24:39.950 --> 00:24:41.530 align:middle line:90%
namely, root in d--

00:24:41.530 --> 00:24:44.820 align:middle line:90%
with two different openings.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:59.020 align:middle line:84%
So suppose there exists a
poly-size adversary that does

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:04.180 align:middle line:84%
find a collision such that
the probability that A--

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:05.770 align:middle line:90%
he gets a hash key.

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:13.630 align:middle line:84%
Hash key is from gen. And
somehow, he manages to output

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:16.940 align:middle line:84%
a hash value,
which is root in d.

00:25:16.940 --> 00:25:19.450 align:middle line:90%
That's just a hash value.

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:24.940 align:middle line:90%
And he gives me some index i.

00:25:24.940 --> 00:25:27.530 align:middle line:84%
He managed to open it
in two different way.

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:32.000 align:middle line:84%
He gives me an opening
that says i is 0.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:37.120 align:middle line:84%
And he gives me another opening
that says the i-th bit is 1

00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:41.920 align:middle line:84%
and such that for both of
them, such that for every B,

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:47.260 align:middle line:84%
for both 0 and 1,
ver will accept--

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:56.140 align:middle line:84%
ver and hash key root in
d, i, and row B outputs 1.

00:25:56.140 --> 00:26:00.790 align:middle line:84%
So suppose I have an
algorithm that gives me

00:26:00.790 --> 00:26:04.660 align:middle line:84%
a hash value, an index, and
a valid openings, both a

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:06.020 align:middle line:90%
0 and to 1.

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:09.650 align:middle line:84%
Valid-- I mean that the ver
will accept both of them.

00:26:09.650 --> 00:26:11.680 align:middle line:90%
Oh, sorry-- B.

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:18.010 align:middle line:84%
So ver takes hash key of value,
an index i, and a B, big B,

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:22.760 align:middle line:84%
saying whether index i is 0
or 1, and a proof, an opening.

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:25.970 align:middle line:90%
And suppose he accepts both.

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:28.920 align:middle line:84%
Suppose he accepts both
with probability at least,

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:31.350 align:middle line:90%
I don't know, epsilon.

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:34.073 align:middle line:84%
So suppose there's this
and exists epsilon.

00:26:34.073 --> 00:26:41.885 align:middle line:90%


00:26:41.885 --> 00:26:43.850 align:middle line:84%
So suppose there exists
epsilon such that

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:46.190 align:middle line:84%
for any lambda, the
probability that A

00:26:46.190 --> 00:26:50.210 align:middle line:84%
managed to find the collision
like this is at least epsilon.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:53.030 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to argue
then you can find

00:26:53.030 --> 00:26:55.850 align:middle line:84%
a collision in the
underlying h, in the small h,

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:58.580 align:middle line:90%
with probability epsilon.

00:26:58.580 --> 00:27:01.910 align:middle line:84%
So if you have an
algorithm like this,

00:27:01.910 --> 00:27:18.860 align:middle line:84%
then there exists an algorithm
poly size such that B--

00:27:18.860 --> 00:27:22.940 align:middle line:84%
such that the
probability that B--

00:27:22.940 --> 00:27:29.240 align:middle line:84%
he gets hash key-- the
probability that he outputs,

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:38.240 align:middle line:84%
I know x0, x, and x, prime such
that the original eval hk, x

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:46.190 align:middle line:84%
equals eval hk, x prime,
and x is different

00:27:46.190 --> 00:27:50.900 align:middle line:84%
than x prime, is
at least epsilon.

00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:53.550 align:middle line:84%
But we assume that this
is collision resistance.

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:56.660 align:middle line:84%
We started with an
underlying hash function

00:27:56.660 --> 00:27:58.230 align:middle line:90%
that's collision-resistant.

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:02.717 align:middle line:84%
So the epsilon has
to be negligible.

00:28:02.717 --> 00:28:04.050 align:middle line:90%
That's what we're going to show.

00:28:04.050 --> 00:28:07.730 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to use
if an adversary can

00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:13.830 align:middle line:84%
find a local opening that
collides for the Merkle hash,

00:28:13.830 --> 00:28:16.700 align:middle line:84%
we can use them
to actually break

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:20.810 align:middle line:84%
the defined collision in
the underlying 2 lambda

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:26.150 align:middle line:84%
to lambda hash function in
the little hash function.

00:28:26.150 --> 00:28:27.900 align:middle line:90%
So how does B work?

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:28.950 align:middle line:90%
What does B do?

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:30.660 align:middle line:90%
B takes a hash key.

00:28:30.660 --> 00:28:34.820 align:middle line:84%
He just runs A. So let
me-- so we claim that there

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:36.815 align:middle line:90%
exists a B. So let see.

00:28:36.815 --> 00:28:41.370 align:middle line:84%
B, an input hash
key, what does he do?

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:54.260 align:middle line:84%
Just run A. So get root D, I,
row 0, row 1, which is just A

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:56.840 align:middle line:90%
and hash key.

00:28:56.840 --> 00:29:00.420 align:middle line:90%
Now, he got row 0 and row 1.

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:02.130 align:middle line:90%
What are row 0, row 1?

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:07.440 align:middle line:84%
Each row is this kind of
two pairs of siblings.

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:09.440 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to assume that
both exist because it's

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:10.320 align:middle line:90%
just easier for me.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:11.700 align:middle line:90%
That's why I wrote both of them.

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:21.000 align:middle line:84%
So each opening-- so row 0 is
for each level you have Z0,

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:32.540 align:middle line:90%
J from 0 to D. And row 1 is--

00:29:32.540 --> 00:29:40.130 align:middle line:90%
let's call it Z1, J.

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:41.495 align:middle line:90%
Now, what do we know?

00:29:41.495 --> 00:29:50.760 align:middle line:84%
In the leaves, so we know that
Z0, 0 is different than Z1, 0.

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:54.660 align:middle line:84%
The two leaves are different
because, in one leaf--

00:29:54.660 --> 00:29:57.630 align:middle line:84%
suppose verifier
accepts both of them.

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:00.830 align:middle line:84%
In the case that these
are both accepted,

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:03.840 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to argue that
I found collisions.

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:06.990 align:middle line:84%
So now suppose row 0
and row 1 are accepted.

00:30:06.990 --> 00:30:12.800 align:middle line:90%
So ver accepts both of them.

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:16.320 align:middle line:84%
Now, if ver accepts
both of them--

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:19.380 align:middle line:84%
so we know that the leaves
are different because,

00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:22.590 align:middle line:84%
in the leaves, in one
case, I have here a 1,

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:24.630 align:middle line:84%
in the other case,
I have here a 0.

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:26.920 align:middle line:90%
So they have to be different.

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:29.070 align:middle line:84%
So I know the leaves
are different.

00:30:29.070 --> 00:30:38.825 align:middle line:84%
I also know that Z0, D equals
Z1, D. These are both a root.

00:30:38.825 --> 00:30:40.950 align:middle line:84%
Now, of course, I said, we
don't need to include it

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:43.890 align:middle line:84%
because-- but I'm just
putting here because I want--

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:46.260 align:middle line:84%
the reason I want to include
it is just for clarity

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:51.420 align:middle line:84%
to say, look, we have D layers
of information on each opening.

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:53.730 align:middle line:84%
The leaves, they must
be different because one

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:55.290 align:middle line:90%
opens to 0, one opens to 1.

00:30:55.290 --> 00:30:57.640 align:middle line:84%
The root, the left
one must be the same.

00:30:57.640 --> 00:30:58.950 align:middle line:90%
It's the root.

00:30:58.950 --> 00:31:03.480 align:middle line:84%
It means that there must be
some kind of two adjacent layers

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:04.920 align:middle line:90%
on which one of--

00:31:04.920 --> 00:31:08.380 align:middle line:84%
below they disagree
and above they agree.

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:10.710 align:middle line:84%
We know here they
disagree, here they agree.

00:31:10.710 --> 00:31:11.308 align:middle line:90%
They disagree.

00:31:11.308 --> 00:31:12.850 align:middle line:84%
Do they disagree
here, disagree here?

00:31:12.850 --> 00:31:14.392 align:middle line:84%
At some point they're
going to agree.

00:31:14.392 --> 00:31:23.190 align:middle line:84%
So we say there must be a
layer J such that Zj0 is

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:35.130 align:middle line:84%
different than Zj1, But Zj plus
1, 0 is equal to Zj plus 1, 1.

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:35.970 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:31:35.970 --> 00:31:37.750 align:middle line:90%
OK, they disagree here.

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:39.010 align:middle line:90%
How about one layer above?

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:39.775 align:middle line:90%
Do they agree?

00:31:39.775 --> 00:31:40.650 align:middle line:90%
If they agree, great.

00:31:40.650 --> 00:31:42.400 align:middle line:84%
Then that's going
to be our j plus 1.

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:43.150 align:middle line:90%
They disagree?

00:31:43.150 --> 00:31:45.267 align:middle line:84%
Let's go up until,
at some point,

00:31:45.267 --> 00:31:47.350 align:middle line:84%
they'll agree because,
Hamiltonian, do they agree.

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:47.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:51.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So just for the video,
should it be 0 j [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:53.735 align:middle line:90%
and 0 j plus 1?

00:31:53.735 --> 00:31:55.360 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Oh,
thank you very much.

00:31:55.360 --> 00:31:57.220 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:31:57.220 --> 00:31:57.720 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:31:57.720 --> 00:31:58.150 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:31:58.150 --> 00:32:00.525 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, it's not just for the
video, it's also for you guys

00:32:00.525 --> 00:32:04.990 align:middle line:84%
so I won't confuse
you more than that.

00:32:04.990 --> 00:32:06.990 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:32:06.990 --> 00:32:10.720 align:middle line:90%
1, j, 1, j plus 1.

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:12.480 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.550 align:middle line:90%
OK, yes.

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:16.620 align:middle line:90%
Is it--

00:32:16.620 --> 00:32:18.330 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is that [INAUDIBLE]?

00:32:18.330 --> 00:32:20.220 align:middle line:90%
Because j plus 1 is--

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:21.480 align:middle line:90%
oh.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:22.680 align:middle line:90%
You missed j plus 1.

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:23.370 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Did I miss--

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:24.620 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You missed j plus 1.

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:25.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: On the bottom.

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:27.190 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Yes.

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:28.215 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You need a 0.

00:32:28.215 --> 00:32:29.680 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Oh my God.

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:31.140 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:32:31.140 --> 00:32:33.035 align:middle line:90%
No, the 0, 1.

00:32:33.035 --> 00:32:34.446 align:middle line:90%
Ah!

00:32:34.446 --> 00:32:37.350 align:middle line:90%
OK, did I get it right?

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:38.190 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:42.970 align:middle line:84%
OK, so there's a layer
j such that, in layer j

00:32:42.970 --> 00:32:45.690 align:middle line:84%
they disagree, in layer
j plus 1 they agree.

00:32:45.690 --> 00:32:46.980 align:middle line:90%
That's it.

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:48.180 align:middle line:90%
This is a collision.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:49.060 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:32:49.060 --> 00:32:51.270 align:middle line:90%
What is layer j plus 1?

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:55.950 align:middle line:84%
One of them is the
father, is the parent.

00:32:55.950 --> 00:33:00.840 align:middle line:84%
So we have two
that they disagree.

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:04.020 align:middle line:90%
But both of these they agree on.

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:06.060 align:middle line:84%
In particular,
they agree on this.

00:33:06.060 --> 00:33:08.320 align:middle line:84%
So then, so we
found a collision.

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:21.520 align:middle line:84%
This must mean that the
hash eval hash key of Z0j

00:33:21.520 --> 00:33:27.850 align:middle line:84%
is equal to eval
hash key of Z1j.

00:33:27.850 --> 00:33:34.990 align:middle line:84%
Because this value is one
of this, and one of this

00:33:34.990 --> 00:33:38.352 align:middle line:84%
depending if it's the left or
the right depending on the tree,

00:33:38.352 --> 00:33:39.620 align:middle line:90%
and they're both equal.

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:40.340 align:middle line:90%
So done.

00:33:40.340 --> 00:33:49.130 align:middle line:84%
I found a collision with
the same exact probability.

00:33:49.130 --> 00:33:52.030 align:middle line:90%
So it's--

00:33:52.030 --> 00:33:53.500 align:middle line:90%
Now, as I said, this--

00:33:53.500 --> 00:33:56.740 align:middle line:84%
I wrote here, suppose
if this poly lambda,

00:33:56.740 --> 00:33:59.950 align:middle line:84%
I could have-- if we assumed
that the underlying hash

00:33:59.950 --> 00:34:03.940 align:middle line:84%
function is t secure, namely
you can't find collisions

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:06.770 align:middle line:84%
even if you run time
poly t of lambda,

00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:11.710 align:middle line:84%
then we get that the Merkle
hash is poly t secure because B,

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:14.188 align:middle line:84%
all it does really
is just run A.

00:34:14.188 --> 00:34:17.560 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't run much more than A,
it just runs A and then it does

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:18.949 align:middle line:90%
something very trivial.

00:34:18.949 --> 00:34:20.929 align:middle line:84%
So the complexity of
breaking the Merkle hash

00:34:20.929 --> 00:34:22.929 align:middle line:84%
is really the same as the
complexity of breaking

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:25.179 align:middle line:90%
the underlying hash.

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:28.079 align:middle line:84%
So if the underlying hash is t
secure, Merkle hash is t secure.

00:34:28.079 --> 00:34:32.800 align:middle line:90%


00:34:32.800 --> 00:34:33.620 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:39.920 align:middle line:90%


00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:40.850 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:34:40.850 --> 00:34:43.100 align:middle line:90%
So where are we?

00:34:43.100 --> 00:34:44.900 align:middle line:90%
So we proved the Merkle hash.

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:46.780 align:middle line:84%
As we saw, the
Merkle hash was used

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:50.020 align:middle line:84%
to give an interactive-- a
succinct interactive argument

00:34:50.020 --> 00:34:51.790 align:middle line:90%
for all of NP.

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:56.511 align:middle line:90%
So now, if you're willing to--

00:34:56.511 --> 00:35:01.460 align:middle line:84%
you're happy with interactive
protocols, thank you very much.

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:03.675 align:middle line:84%
I hope you enjoyed the
semester, and we're done.

00:35:03.675 --> 00:35:04.960 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:07.810 align:middle line:84%
If you're not though-- but,
as I said, we're actually--

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:10.150 align:middle line:84%
we're not happy with
interactive protocols.

00:35:10.150 --> 00:35:11.570 align:middle line:84%
It's really
interesting, actually,

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:13.240 align:middle line:84%
to think of the
evolution of this

00:35:13.240 --> 00:35:18.700 align:middle line:84%
because before all this
interactive proofs and arguments

00:35:18.700 --> 00:35:22.060 align:middle line:84%
and so on, we had just
mathematical proofs, which

00:35:22.060 --> 00:35:26.170 align:middle line:84%
were not interactive, but
they were very, very long.

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:27.620 align:middle line:90%
And people were not happy.

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:29.150 align:middle line:90%
It's like, oh, they're so long.

00:35:29.150 --> 00:35:31.520 align:middle line:84%
And then interactive
proofs were defined.

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:34.630 align:middle line:84%
I think they were defined only
for the sake of zero knowledge.

00:35:34.630 --> 00:35:37.000 align:middle line:84%
They were defined just to
get zero knowledge proofs.

00:35:37.000 --> 00:35:38.500 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to actually
mention that--

00:35:38.500 --> 00:35:41.590 align:middle line:84%
we're going to talk about
zero knowledge later today.

00:35:41.590 --> 00:35:44.650 align:middle line:84%
And this motivated
the interest--

00:35:44.650 --> 00:35:46.450 align:middle line:90%
to introduce interaction.

00:35:46.450 --> 00:35:48.282 align:middle line:84%
And then people are
like, wow, you--

00:35:48.282 --> 00:35:50.240 align:middle line:84%
oh, what a great-- forget
about zero knowledge.

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:51.140 align:middle line:90%
Forget about cryptography.

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:52.360 align:middle line:90%
This is such a great model.

00:35:52.360 --> 00:35:56.270 align:middle line:84%
Using interaction we
can prove a lot more.

00:35:56.270 --> 00:35:57.590 align:middle line:90%
And we saw the GKR protocol.

00:35:57.590 --> 00:36:02.680 align:middle line:84%
You can do-- you can prove,
actually, any circuits

00:36:02.680 --> 00:36:05.440 align:middle line:84%
that are really, really huge
as long as they're kind of

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:09.440 align:middle line:84%
have that, you can do P
space or any computation that

00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:10.430 align:middle line:90%
has poly depth.

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:12.660 align:middle line:84%
Your verifier can be
any depth D. You can--

00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:15.540 align:middle line:84%
the verifier runs
in time D. Great.

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:17.255 align:middle line:84%
Not-- it doesn't
run in the side,

00:36:17.255 --> 00:36:18.630 align:middle line:84%
so it's much, much
more powerful.

00:36:18.630 --> 00:36:18.740 align:middle line:90%
Wow.

00:36:18.740 --> 00:36:18.920 align:middle line:90%
Wow.

00:36:18.920 --> 00:36:19.560 align:middle line:90%
Wow.

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:21.860 align:middle line:84%
And then people are like,
actually, with cryptography,

00:36:21.860 --> 00:36:23.015 align:middle line:90%
you can do much more.

00:36:23.015 --> 00:36:23.515 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:36:23.515 --> 00:36:24.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah!

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:26.520 align:middle line:84%
But now, but wait,
there was a big price.

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:28.520 align:middle line:84%
We actually don't
want interaction.

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:29.550 align:middle line:90%
So where are we.

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:32.270 align:middle line:90%
Are we back at NP?

00:36:32.270 --> 00:36:35.150 align:middle line:84%
NP is very-- we're back in
the mathematical proofs that

00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:37.010 align:middle line:90%
are way, way too long?

00:36:37.010 --> 00:36:40.460 align:middle line:84%
So I said, no,
we're actually going

00:36:40.460 --> 00:36:42.690 align:middle line:84%
to circle back in a
very interesting way

00:36:42.690 --> 00:36:44.905 align:middle line:84%
and get succinct
non-interactive proofs.

00:36:44.905 --> 00:36:47.600 align:middle line:90%


00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:49.265 align:middle line:90%
So how do we circle back?

00:36:49.265 --> 00:37:08.990 align:middle line:90%


00:37:08.990 --> 00:37:13.040 align:middle line:84%
So this idea of how to
circle back actually

00:37:13.040 --> 00:37:19.170 align:middle line:84%
was put forth by
Fiat and Shamir.

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:22.640 align:middle line:90%
This is in the mid '80s, In '86.

00:37:22.640 --> 00:37:25.830 align:middle line:84%
They proposed a really,
really nice paradigm.

00:37:25.830 --> 00:37:29.090 align:middle line:84%
Actually, they didn't think
about-- this is, remember,

00:37:29.090 --> 00:37:29.790 align:middle line:90%
this is '86.

00:37:29.790 --> 00:37:32.190 align:middle line:84%
This is before
interactive proofs.

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:35.510 align:middle line:84%
It was just when zero
knowledge was introduced.

00:37:35.510 --> 00:37:38.340 align:middle line:84%
Interactive proofs
just came about,

00:37:38.340 --> 00:37:41.520 align:middle line:84%
but now there was no PCP,
no interactive argument,

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:43.040 align:middle line:90%
none of that.

00:37:43.040 --> 00:37:45.130 align:middle line:90%
This is in '86, Fiat and Shamir.

00:37:45.130 --> 00:37:56.180 align:middle line:90%


00:37:56.180 --> 00:37:59.540 align:middle line:84%
They proposed a
method of actually

00:37:59.540 --> 00:38:02.700 align:middle line:84%
reducing interaction-- of
constructing signature schemes.

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:08.000 align:middle line:84%
Now, I stand behind what I said,
that this class will not require

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:10.247 align:middle line:90%
a lot of crypto background.

00:38:10.247 --> 00:38:12.330 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to know
what signature schemes are.

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:18.350 align:middle line:84%
But what they did, their idea
is, there is a primitive called

00:38:18.350 --> 00:38:19.830 align:middle line:90%
identification scheme.

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:22.372 align:middle line:84%
I want to prove to
you that I'm Yael.

00:38:22.372 --> 00:38:23.580 align:middle line:90%
How do I prove that I'm Yael?

00:38:23.580 --> 00:38:25.740 align:middle line:84%
I have a public key
corresponding to my name.

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:28.115 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to prove to you
that I know the secret key.

00:38:28.115 --> 00:38:30.058 align:middle line:84%
Me knowing-- whoever
knows the secret key has

00:38:30.058 --> 00:38:31.850 align:middle line:84%
my identity, essentially,
that's how things

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:33.180 align:middle line:90%
work in the digital world.

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:35.790 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to prove to you
that I know the secret key.

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:38.360 align:middle line:90%
This proves that I'm me.

00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:40.550 align:middle line:90%
This proof is interactive.

00:38:40.550 --> 00:38:43.830 align:middle line:84%
Identification protocols are
like three-round protocols.

00:38:43.830 --> 00:38:46.830 align:middle line:84%
I give you a message alpha,
you send me a question beta,

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:48.930 align:middle line:84%
I give you an answer
gamma, you verify.

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:50.900 align:middle line:90%
That's how it works.

00:38:50.900 --> 00:38:54.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, they said, let's convert
this identification scheme

00:38:54.740 --> 00:38:56.570 align:middle line:90%
into a signature scheme.

00:38:56.570 --> 00:38:59.570 align:middle line:84%
Instead of interacting
to make sure it's you,

00:38:59.570 --> 00:39:04.720 align:middle line:84%
every time you sign-- you
want to send a message,

00:39:04.720 --> 00:39:08.730 align:middle line:84%
use this message, take this
identification protocol together

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:11.440 align:middle line:84%
with the message and
eliminate the interaction.

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:12.940 align:middle line:84%
I'll explain exactly
how they do it.

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:15.820 align:middle line:84%
But they do it in a way to
construct signature scheme.

00:39:15.820 --> 00:39:17.820 align:middle line:90%
That was their goal.

00:39:17.820 --> 00:39:21.810 align:middle line:84%
But now, after several years
after, we use it all the time

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:24.900 align:middle line:84%
to actually forget about
just the specific application

00:39:24.900 --> 00:39:26.620 align:middle line:84%
of identification
scheme to signature,

00:39:26.620 --> 00:39:27.820 align:middle line:90%
that's one application.

00:39:27.820 --> 00:39:31.170 align:middle line:84%
But actually we can show--
we can use it to reduce,

00:39:31.170 --> 00:39:34.860 align:middle line:84%
to eliminate interaction, not
only from identification scheme,

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:39.190 align:middle line:84%
but from any interactive
protocol that is public coin.

00:39:39.190 --> 00:39:40.480 align:middle line:90%
So let me explain.

00:39:40.480 --> 00:39:44.190 align:middle line:90%
So now, take any protocol.

00:39:44.190 --> 00:40:04.830 align:middle line:84%
So this is a paradigm for
eliminating interaction

00:40:04.830 --> 00:40:15.330 align:middle line:84%
from interactive protocol
that are public coin.

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:19.180 align:middle line:84%
So not all protocol, but
ones that are public coin.

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:22.560 align:middle line:84%
So what is a public
coin protocol?

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:25.090 align:middle line:84%
Take any protocol,
let's say, a proof.

00:40:25.090 --> 00:40:28.770 align:middle line:84%
So I have a prover
and a verifier.

00:40:28.770 --> 00:40:30.540 align:middle line:90%
And suppose for a second--

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:34.390 align:middle line:84%
so suppose for simplicity
that it's three messages.

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:37.570 align:middle line:84%
So the verifier-- the prover
sends a message alpha.

00:40:37.570 --> 00:40:41.435 align:middle line:84%
The verifier sends
beta, which is random.

00:40:41.435 --> 00:40:43.950 align:middle line:84%
Public coin means the
message of the verifier

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:46.030 align:middle line:90%
is completely random.

00:40:46.030 --> 00:40:48.720 align:middle line:90%
Let's say it's lambda bits.

00:40:48.720 --> 00:40:51.833 align:middle line:84%
If it's less, then just
pad it to be lambda.

00:40:51.833 --> 00:40:53.500 align:middle line:84%
And if it's more,
just call that lambda.

00:40:53.500 --> 00:40:55.000 align:middle line:84%
Make that the
security parameter.

00:40:55.000 --> 00:40:58.240 align:middle line:84%
Just increase the
security parameter.

00:40:58.240 --> 00:41:01.410 align:middle line:90%
Now you send gamma.

00:41:01.410 --> 00:41:02.680 align:middle line:90%
And maybe it's more round.

00:41:02.680 --> 00:41:08.130 align:middle line:84%
So maybe then he sends
delta, let's say random bits,

00:41:08.130 --> 00:41:12.150 align:middle line:84%
and then you say epsilon,
and you can continue.

00:41:12.150 --> 00:41:15.100 align:middle line:84%
Let me stop here
for the sake of--

00:41:15.100 --> 00:41:18.370 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm going to
eliminate interaction.

00:41:18.370 --> 00:41:20.460 align:middle line:90%
How do we eliminate interaction?

00:41:20.460 --> 00:41:22.410 align:middle line:84%
It's an interaction,
it's very important.

00:41:22.410 --> 00:41:23.880 align:middle line:90%
I mean, the entire--

00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:28.140 align:middle line:84%
if you remember the
sumcheck in GKR--

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:32.580 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's tempting maybe
to say, oh, tell the prover,

00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:34.915 align:middle line:84%
compute alpha, beta,
gamma, just compute

00:41:34.915 --> 00:41:36.040 align:middle line:90%
the transcript on your own.

00:41:36.040 --> 00:41:40.890 align:middle line:84%
You simulate the verifier's
messages, then he'll cheat.

00:41:40.890 --> 00:41:44.640 align:middle line:84%
So how do you get rid
of the interaction?

00:41:44.640 --> 00:41:49.440 align:middle line:90%
It seems really, really hard.

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:51.010 align:middle line:90%
Actually, it's so easy.

00:41:51.010 --> 00:41:52.920 align:middle line:90%
I mean, their idea is trivial.

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:54.160 align:middle line:90%
So what is their idea?

00:41:54.160 --> 00:41:57.900 align:middle line:90%
They say the following.

00:41:57.900 --> 00:42:01.900 align:middle line:84%
How does the verifier-- how
does the prover get this beta?

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:04.180 align:middle line:90%
The verifier chose it.

00:42:04.180 --> 00:42:05.830 align:middle line:90%
Do you know what?

00:42:05.830 --> 00:42:09.375 align:middle line:84%
Let's not have the ver-- now
we don't have a verifier.

00:42:09.375 --> 00:42:10.250 align:middle line:90%
It's not interactive.

00:42:10.250 --> 00:42:11.300 align:middle line:90%
There is no verifier.

00:42:11.300 --> 00:42:13.240 align:middle line:90%
So how do you compute this beta?

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:16.840 align:middle line:84%
Let's compute it as a
hash function applied

00:42:16.840 --> 00:42:19.870 align:middle line:90%
to the transcript so far.

00:42:19.870 --> 00:42:21.440 align:middle line:90%
So let me explain.

00:42:21.440 --> 00:42:24.790 align:middle line:84%
So how-- here's
how you convert it

00:42:24.790 --> 00:42:30.460 align:middle line:84%
to P Fiat-Shamir,
V Fiat-Shamir, this

00:42:30.460 --> 00:42:34.390 align:middle line:84%
is associated with
some hash family.

00:42:34.390 --> 00:42:36.950 align:middle line:84%
There are some-- it's
associated with a hash family.

00:42:36.950 --> 00:42:40.360 align:middle line:84%
So the Fiat-Shamir paradigm, you
can take any hash family and use

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:42.220 align:middle line:90%
that to reduce interaction.

00:42:42.220 --> 00:42:44.180 align:middle line:84%
How do you use it to
reduce interaction?

00:42:44.180 --> 00:42:50.600 align:middle line:84%
Now, there are some hash key
that's chosen once and for all.

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:51.770 align:middle line:90%
Everybody knows it.

00:42:51.770 --> 00:42:53.540 align:middle line:84%
Either the verifier
sends it once,

00:42:53.540 --> 00:42:56.120 align:middle line:84%
or if you think about
public-- everybody can verify,

00:42:56.120 --> 00:43:00.980 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, the US government
publishes this hash key,

00:43:00.980 --> 00:43:02.440 align:middle line:90%
everybody uses this hash key.

00:43:02.440 --> 00:43:04.610 align:middle line:84%
There are some fixed hash
key, everybody uses it.

00:43:04.610 --> 00:43:10.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, once we agree on a hash
key, what does the prover do?

00:43:10.930 --> 00:43:12.610 align:middle line:90%
He computes alpha.

00:43:12.610 --> 00:43:17.860 align:middle line:84%
Let's say he wants to prove
that x is in some language.

00:43:17.860 --> 00:43:20.830 align:middle line:84%
So let's say he says, OK, I'm
going to prove to you that x

00:43:20.830 --> 00:43:22.180 align:middle line:90%
is in the language.

00:43:22.180 --> 00:43:24.040 align:middle line:90%
Let me prove it to you.

00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:26.530 align:middle line:90%
OK, I compute the alpha.

00:43:26.530 --> 00:43:28.640 align:middle line:90%
Now I'm waiting for a beta.

00:43:28.640 --> 00:43:30.320 align:middle line:90%
But there is no verifier.

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:31.548 align:middle line:90%
Fine.

00:43:31.548 --> 00:43:32.840 align:middle line:90%
I'll tell you what the beta is.

00:43:32.840 --> 00:43:36.250 align:middle line:84%
Beta is just-- has to--
is going to be eval

00:43:36.250 --> 00:43:42.730 align:middle line:84%
of hash key and the transcript
so far, x and alpha.

00:43:42.730 --> 00:43:46.210 align:middle line:84%
That's currently what's in
the transcript, the statement

00:43:46.210 --> 00:43:48.520 align:middle line:90%
and the first message alpha.

00:43:48.520 --> 00:43:50.500 align:middle line:84%
Now the proof is like,
OK, this is the beta

00:43:50.500 --> 00:43:51.770 align:middle line:90%
I got from the verifier.

00:43:51.770 --> 00:43:55.180 align:middle line:84%
He's interpreting this as the
beta he got from the verifier.

00:43:55.180 --> 00:43:59.540 align:middle line:84%
And now he computes gamma, my
answer, the prover's answer.

00:43:59.540 --> 00:44:01.840 align:middle line:84%
Now he's waiting for a
message for-- a query

00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:04.990 align:middle line:90%
from the verifier, some delta.

00:44:04.990 --> 00:44:06.610 align:middle line:84%
But there is no
verifier, so it's

00:44:06.610 --> 00:44:12.970 align:middle line:84%
going to compute the delta
as eval of the hash key using

00:44:12.970 --> 00:44:14.150 align:middle line:90%
the transcript so far.

00:44:14.150 --> 00:44:19.750 align:middle line:90%
So x, alpha, beta, gamma.

00:44:19.750 --> 00:44:23.890 align:middle line:84%
Once he has delta, he's going
to go back to being a prover

00:44:23.890 --> 00:44:27.850 align:middle line:90%
and compute epsilon.

00:44:27.850 --> 00:44:29.420 align:middle line:90%
And then you can continue.

00:44:29.420 --> 00:44:30.790 align:middle line:90%
Next message, he goes--

00:44:30.790 --> 00:44:34.450 align:middle line:84%
computes a hash value
of all the transcript

00:44:34.450 --> 00:44:37.770 align:middle line:84%
so far, computes the
next prover's message.

00:44:37.770 --> 00:44:43.090 align:middle line:84%
Here another verif-- and we
go-- that's how we continue.

00:44:43.090 --> 00:44:45.500 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about--
so what did we go?

00:44:45.500 --> 00:44:49.090 align:middle line:84%
We started from an
interactive protocol.

00:44:49.090 --> 00:44:51.320 align:middle line:90%
You can have many rounds.

00:44:51.320 --> 00:44:54.785 align:middle line:84%
And now all we need is
to agree on a hash key.

00:44:54.785 --> 00:44:57.410 align:middle line:84%
And once again, we're going to
hash key-- the prover just gives

00:44:57.410 --> 00:44:58.980 align:middle line:90%
you-- this is one message.

00:44:58.980 --> 00:45:01.160 align:middle line:90%
That's it.

00:45:01.160 --> 00:45:01.880 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:45:01.880 --> 00:45:03.685 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is it important to
include beta in the transcript

00:45:03.685 --> 00:45:04.430 align:middle line:90%
when you're hashing?

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:05.670 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Good,
great question.

00:45:05.670 --> 00:45:06.630 align:middle line:90%
Is it important to include beta?

00:45:06.630 --> 00:45:08.297 align:middle line:84%
Actually, you don't
need to include beta

00:45:08.297 --> 00:45:10.520 align:middle line:84%
at all because the
verifier can compute beta.

00:45:10.520 --> 00:45:14.640 align:middle line:84%
So you only need to know beta
so you can compute gamma.

00:45:14.640 --> 00:45:17.030 align:middle line:84%
You actually don't
need to include it.

00:45:17.030 --> 00:45:17.693 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

00:45:17.693 --> 00:45:18.610 align:middle line:90%
Great, great question.

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:22.220 align:middle line:90%


00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:25.790 align:middle line:84%
So note, for example, if you
think about Kilian and Micali

00:45:25.790 --> 00:45:28.140 align:middle line:84%
protocol, you may
not remember it,

00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:30.760 align:middle line:84%
but let me just write it here
just to refresh your memory.

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:31.260 align:middle line:90%
Why not?

00:45:31.260 --> 00:45:33.052 align:middle line:84%
You don't need to
actually understand this,

00:45:33.052 --> 00:45:36.290 align:middle line:84%
but since it's such a
nice protocol-- and so,

00:45:36.290 --> 00:45:37.050 align:middle line:90%
how does it go?

00:45:37.050 --> 00:45:38.790 align:middle line:90%
There's a prover and a verifier.

00:45:38.790 --> 00:45:40.650 align:middle line:90%
The verifier sends a hash key.

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:44.210 align:middle line:84%
So he wants to prove
that x is in L. He

00:45:44.210 --> 00:45:47.280 align:middle line:90%
does a Merkle hash of the PC.

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:48.660 align:middle line:90%
So he computes a PCP.

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:56.130 align:middle line:84%
Pi is a PCP for x in L. And
he sends you the Merkle hash.

00:45:56.130 --> 00:46:02.340 align:middle line:84%
So he sends you
root and the depth.

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:07.220 align:middle line:84%
That's just the--
so he takes the PCP

00:46:07.220 --> 00:46:09.595 align:middle line:84%
and he does a hash
with local opening.

00:46:09.595 --> 00:46:10.970 align:middle line:84%
For example, Merkle
hash actually

00:46:10.970 --> 00:46:13.800 align:middle line:84%
can do any hash with local
opening that you want.

00:46:13.800 --> 00:46:15.950 align:middle line:90%
This is the most common one.

00:46:15.950 --> 00:46:21.060 align:middle line:84%
And then he gets the randomness
from the PCP verifier.

00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:24.140 align:middle line:84%
So this is r from
r corresponding

00:46:24.140 --> 00:46:26.360 align:middle line:90%
to the PCP verifier.

00:46:26.360 --> 00:46:29.100 align:middle line:84%
This determines a few
locations to open.

00:46:29.100 --> 00:46:30.240 align:middle line:90%
So now he opens.

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:36.320 align:middle line:84%
So he gives-- so this randomness
corresponds to some locations,

00:46:36.320 --> 00:46:39.860 align:middle line:84%
i1 up to il, and then
you just open pi i1

00:46:39.860 --> 00:46:45.230 align:middle line:84%
with open 1 up to
pi il with open l.

00:46:45.230 --> 00:46:46.650 align:middle line:90%
That's the protocol.

00:46:46.650 --> 00:46:50.900 align:middle line:90%
Note, this is completely random.

00:46:50.900 --> 00:46:53.690 align:middle line:84%
It's the randomness
of the PCP verifier.

00:46:53.690 --> 00:46:56.610 align:middle line:90%
So we can eliminate interaction.

00:46:56.610 --> 00:46:58.550 align:middle line:84%
This, if you think
of it, this is just

00:46:58.550 --> 00:47:01.820 align:middle line:84%
of a protocol where
you send a hash key,

00:47:01.820 --> 00:47:04.605 align:middle line:84%
and then you have
alpha, beta, gamma.

00:47:04.605 --> 00:47:08.480 align:middle line:90%


00:47:08.480 --> 00:47:10.260 align:middle line:90%
So let's eliminate interaction.

00:47:10.260 --> 00:47:12.090 align:middle line:84%
So how do we
eliminate interaction?

00:47:12.090 --> 00:47:14.720 align:middle line:90%
Well, there's this hash key.

00:47:14.720 --> 00:47:16.640 align:middle line:84%
We can use this hash
key to do Fiat-Shamir,

00:47:16.640 --> 00:47:21.020 align:middle line:84%
but we can also use another hash
key, hash key sub Fiat-Shamir.

00:47:21.020 --> 00:47:26.970 align:middle line:84%
And now the-- well, this
can be-- now you give alpha.

00:47:26.970 --> 00:47:29.820 align:middle line:84%
Beta is going to be simply--
you don't have to include it.

00:47:29.820 --> 00:47:31.400 align:middle line:84%
But it's going to
be, in our head,

00:47:31.400 --> 00:47:42.080 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be eval of hash
key Fiat-Shamir of x in alpha.

00:47:42.080 --> 00:47:46.520 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe hash key is
also part of the transcript.

00:47:46.520 --> 00:47:48.870 align:middle line:90%
And this gives beta.

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:50.905 align:middle line:84%
Now you compute the gamma,
which is the opening.

00:47:50.905 --> 00:47:54.290 align:middle line:90%


00:47:54.290 --> 00:47:59.480 align:middle line:84%
So you can take
any-- so now if--

00:47:59.480 --> 00:48:01.290 align:middle line:84%
what does the
Fiat-Shamir gives you?

00:48:01.290 --> 00:48:04.460 align:middle line:84%
It gives us a way--
remember, Kilian and Micali

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:06.300 align:middle line:90%
was very succinct.

00:48:06.300 --> 00:48:10.580 align:middle line:84%
You can take x any--
let's say NP language,

00:48:10.580 --> 00:48:11.703 align:middle line:90%
N can be very large.

00:48:11.703 --> 00:48:14.370 align:middle line:84%
It can be lambda to the 1,000, I
don't know, it can be whatever.

00:48:14.370 --> 00:48:16.530 align:middle line:84%
It can be even super
polynomial lambda.

00:48:16.530 --> 00:48:20.780 align:middle line:84%
It can be almost as
large as 2 to the lambda.

00:48:20.780 --> 00:48:22.980 align:middle line:84%
And look how succinct
the protocol is.

00:48:22.980 --> 00:48:26.510 align:middle line:84%
You just send a hash key, the
root, which is lambda bits,

00:48:26.510 --> 00:48:30.920 align:middle line:84%
and the opening, the PCP is all
lambda bits, poly lambda bits,

00:48:30.920 --> 00:48:32.880 align:middle line:90%
so very, very succinct.

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:34.770 align:middle line:84%
And now you can
even just make it--

00:48:34.770 --> 00:48:39.740 align:middle line:84%
so what is this-- you can
all send it all one round.

00:48:39.740 --> 00:48:41.295 align:middle line:90%
So what does it give you?

00:48:41.295 --> 00:48:42.170 align:middle line:90%
What does it tell us?

00:48:42.170 --> 00:48:45.170 align:middle line:84%
That we can take
any NP statement

00:48:45.170 --> 00:48:47.210 align:middle line:84%
and give a proof of
size poly lambda.

00:48:47.210 --> 00:48:48.730 align:middle line:90%
Doesn't matter what size x is.

00:48:48.730 --> 00:48:51.990 align:middle line:90%


00:48:51.990 --> 00:48:54.120 align:middle line:90%
That's amazing.

00:48:54.120 --> 00:48:56.160 align:middle line:84%
Without cryptography, we
need poly [INAUDIBLE],

00:48:56.160 --> 00:48:57.940 align:middle line:90%
these gigantic witnesses.

00:48:57.940 --> 00:48:58.440 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:48:58.440 --> 00:49:00.732 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I guess for the
collision resistance security

00:49:00.732 --> 00:49:03.000 align:middle line:84%
property and probabilistic
encoder hash keys,

00:49:03.000 --> 00:49:05.450 align:middle line:84%
you can imagine some
kind of weird hash family

00:49:05.450 --> 00:49:07.450 align:middle line:84%
where there exist hash
keys that are really bad.

00:49:07.450 --> 00:49:10.080 align:middle line:84%
And so I guess here it's
fine because the verifier

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:11.080 align:middle line:90%
chooses the hash key.

00:49:11.080 --> 00:49:13.800 align:middle line:84%
But you might imagine if the US
government publishes a hash key

00:49:13.800 --> 00:49:16.230 align:middle line:84%
or anything else, what if
it happens to be a bad one

00:49:16.230 --> 00:49:17.650 align:middle line:84%
or it was chosen
to be a bad one?

00:49:17.650 --> 00:49:19.300 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK, two things.

00:49:19.300 --> 00:49:20.760 align:middle line:84%
First, you're
saying, who chooses

00:49:20.760 --> 00:49:22.350 align:middle line:84%
this hash key of
the Fiat-Shamir,

00:49:22.350 --> 00:49:23.940 align:middle line:90%
how do we know that we trust it?

00:49:23.940 --> 00:49:27.060 align:middle line:84%
But you know, it's
much worse than that.

00:49:27.060 --> 00:49:27.760 align:middle line:90%
Is it secure?

00:49:27.760 --> 00:49:31.320 align:middle line:84%
Let's say it's chosen
randomly from the hash family.

00:49:31.320 --> 00:49:34.140 align:middle line:90%
Is this sound?

00:49:34.140 --> 00:49:36.600 align:middle line:90%
So again, what did we do here?

00:49:36.600 --> 00:49:39.610 align:middle line:84%
We started with an
interactive protocol.

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:40.600 align:middle line:90%
We proved it's sound.

00:49:40.600 --> 00:49:41.790 align:middle line:84%
Let's say, Kilian
and Micali is sound.

00:49:41.790 --> 00:49:42.580 align:middle line:90%
We proved it.

00:49:42.580 --> 00:49:44.330 align:middle line:84%
Assuming we have
collision-resistant hash,

00:49:44.330 --> 00:49:46.470 align:middle line:90%
we proved that it's sound.

00:49:46.470 --> 00:49:50.640 align:middle line:84%
It's sound assuming the
prover gets the messages

00:49:50.640 --> 00:49:52.090 align:middle line:90%
of the verifier randomly.

00:49:52.090 --> 00:49:54.070 align:middle line:90%
That's what it sounds.

00:49:54.070 --> 00:49:56.820 align:middle line:90%
We used it to prove soundness.

00:49:56.820 --> 00:49:57.983 align:middle line:90%
Now, what am I saying?

00:49:57.983 --> 00:49:58.900 align:middle line:90%
What are we doing now?

00:49:58.900 --> 00:50:01.240 align:middle line:90%
I'm saying, oh, forget about it.

00:50:01.240 --> 00:50:02.230 align:middle line:90%
It's not random.

00:50:02.230 --> 00:50:07.890 align:middle line:84%
Actually, you compute it on
your own from some hash key

00:50:07.890 --> 00:50:10.200 align:middle line:84%
that someone chooses
even honestly

00:50:10.200 --> 00:50:13.200 align:middle line:84%
from-- let's say we agree-- we
all agreed on some hash family

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:14.948 align:middle line:84%
and someone honestly
chose a hash key.

00:50:14.948 --> 00:50:16.240 align:middle line:90%
But now you know this hash key.

00:50:16.240 --> 00:50:18.300 align:middle line:90%
Now you compute.

00:50:18.300 --> 00:50:19.270 align:middle line:90%
Who says it's sound?

00:50:19.270 --> 00:50:20.380 align:middle line:90%
I mean, is it still sound?

00:50:20.380 --> 00:50:21.580 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it's not sound.

00:50:21.580 --> 00:50:23.580 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Maybe the hash
key always outputs 0,

00:50:23.580 --> 00:50:24.577 align:middle line:90%
but at the start--

00:50:24.577 --> 00:50:25.410 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:50:25.410 --> 00:50:26.160 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK.

00:50:26.160 --> 00:50:34.090 align:middle line:84%
So first of all, of course,
there's-- one can think of--

00:50:34.090 --> 00:50:37.060 align:middle line:84%
this hash functions need
to have some property.

00:50:37.060 --> 00:50:39.330 align:middle line:84%
Collision resistance,
at the minimum,

00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:42.180 align:middle line:84%
if you can find collisions-- for
example, here, you're screwed.

00:50:42.180 --> 00:50:44.400 align:middle line:90%
I mean, it can't be like that.

00:50:44.400 --> 00:50:45.610 align:middle line:90%
It can't be trivial.

00:50:45.610 --> 00:50:46.860 align:middle line:90%
So you need some property.

00:50:46.860 --> 00:50:50.070 align:middle line:84%
Now the question is, what
property do you need?

00:50:50.070 --> 00:50:54.300 align:middle line:84%
What property do you need
from this hash family

00:50:54.300 --> 00:50:55.270 align:middle line:90%
to argue soundness?

00:50:55.270 --> 00:50:56.210 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:50:56.210 --> 00:50:59.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm not sure if
it's pseudorandomness or true

00:50:59.100 --> 00:51:01.237 align:middle line:84%
randomness, but the
output's still random.

00:51:01.237 --> 00:51:02.320 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Very, very.

00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:02.800 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:51:02.800 --> 00:51:04.508 align:middle line:84%
So it seems like,
well, what do you need?

00:51:04.508 --> 00:51:06.940 align:middle line:84%
It needs to be random,
like in that protocol.

00:51:06.940 --> 00:51:08.550 align:middle line:90%
We want to say, take any--

00:51:08.550 --> 00:51:09.490 align:middle line:90%
what is our goal?

00:51:09.490 --> 00:51:13.380 align:middle line:84%
Our goal is to say, take
any protocol that is sound,

00:51:13.380 --> 00:51:18.340 align:middle line:84%
public coin, and we want to
convert it to non-interactive.

00:51:18.340 --> 00:51:19.740 align:middle line:90%
That's what we're trying to do.

00:51:19.740 --> 00:51:21.550 align:middle line:84%
Now, I'm saying, what
property do you need?

00:51:21.550 --> 00:51:24.690 align:middle line:84%
Well, look here,
well, our guarantee

00:51:24.690 --> 00:51:26.380 align:middle line:84%
was that it's sound
if this is random.

00:51:26.380 --> 00:51:30.540 align:middle line:84%
So we need this hash
to be somehow random.

00:51:30.540 --> 00:51:32.520 align:middle line:90%
Stands to reason.

00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:34.150 align:middle line:90%
So this is written out.

00:51:34.150 --> 00:51:36.400 align:middle line:84%
But, look, hash
functions are not random.

00:51:36.400 --> 00:51:37.420 align:middle line:90%
It's a hash function.

00:51:37.420 --> 00:51:40.440 align:middle line:84%
By the way, let me
say, this paradigm

00:51:40.440 --> 00:51:41.980 align:middle line:90%
is used all over the place.

00:51:41.980 --> 00:51:43.690 align:middle line:90%
It's a very popular paradigm.

00:51:43.690 --> 00:51:46.600 align:middle line:84%
It's used a lot in
signature schemes

00:51:46.600 --> 00:51:50.290 align:middle line:90%
and also in proof systems.

00:51:50.290 --> 00:51:52.240 align:middle line:90%
It's used all over the place.

00:51:52.240 --> 00:51:54.680 align:middle line:90%
And which hash function is used?

00:51:54.680 --> 00:51:57.490 align:middle line:84%
Some off-the-shelf
hash function, SHA256,

00:51:57.490 --> 00:52:03.370 align:middle line:84%
like these engineer-type hash
functions that are optimized.

00:52:03.370 --> 00:52:07.455 align:middle line:90%
So is it secure with SHA256?

00:52:07.455 --> 00:52:08.580 align:middle line:90%
It's definitely not random.

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:11.230 align:middle line:90%


00:52:11.230 --> 00:52:13.900 align:middle line:90%
So we actually don't know.

00:52:13.900 --> 00:52:18.100 align:middle line:84%
So let me be honest
that is it secure?

00:52:18.100 --> 00:52:20.030 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:52:20.030 --> 00:52:20.530 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:52:20.530 --> 00:52:21.310 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:52:21.310 --> 00:52:23.230 align:middle line:84%
I think the
specification [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:23.230 --> 00:52:26.140 align:middle line:84%
is already shown that by
length extension [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:26.140 --> 00:52:29.395 align:middle line:84%
is definitely not now
indistinguishable.

00:52:29.395 --> 00:52:30.520 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Yeah, right.

00:52:30.520 --> 00:52:31.030 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:52:31.030 --> 00:52:32.225 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:32.225 --> 00:52:33.100 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Right.

00:52:33.100 --> 00:52:33.280 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:52:33.280 --> 00:52:33.780 align:middle line:90%
Right.

00:52:33.780 --> 00:52:35.260 align:middle line:90%
So actually, let me-- good.

00:52:35.260 --> 00:52:36.630 align:middle line:90%
So let me just say a few things.

00:52:36.630 --> 00:52:39.670 align:middle line:90%


00:52:39.670 --> 00:52:43.600 align:middle line:90%
OK, maybe I'll cut to the chase.

00:52:43.600 --> 00:52:46.880 align:middle line:84%
I do know-- let me tell you,
it's not necessarily secure.

00:52:46.880 --> 00:52:50.050 align:middle line:84%
Actually, unfortunately,
we have counterexamples.

00:52:50.050 --> 00:52:52.760 align:middle line:84%
So I said this is used
in practice all the time.

00:52:52.760 --> 00:52:56.810 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the great
inventions of cryptography.

00:52:56.810 --> 00:52:57.505 align:middle line:90%
Is it secure?

00:52:57.505 --> 00:52:58.005 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:52:58.005 --> 00:53:00.140 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:00.140 --> 00:53:01.480 align:middle line:90%
So what do I mean by no?

00:53:01.480 --> 00:53:04.730 align:middle line:84%
OK, let me-- is
it always secure?

00:53:04.730 --> 00:53:06.100 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:53:06.100 --> 00:53:08.800 align:middle line:84%
So let me say-- let me
take back my notes said.

00:53:08.800 --> 00:53:10.040 align:middle line:90%
Is it always secure?

00:53:10.040 --> 00:53:11.590 align:middle line:84%
Is it always the
case that when you

00:53:11.590 --> 00:53:14.230 align:middle line:84%
start with an
interactive protocol

00:53:14.230 --> 00:53:17.110 align:middle line:84%
and convert a public coin, and
you apply Fiat-Shamir, is it

00:53:17.110 --> 00:53:17.810 align:middle line:90%
always secure?

00:53:17.810 --> 00:53:18.430 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:53:18.430 --> 00:53:21.100 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, let me
tell you, we have

00:53:21.100 --> 00:53:24.820 align:middle line:84%
protocols so that no
matter which hash family

00:53:24.820 --> 00:53:31.990 align:middle line:84%
you use, for all hash families,
the protocol becomes insecure.

00:53:31.990 --> 00:53:33.800 align:middle line:84%
Let me tell you
even more than that.

00:53:33.800 --> 00:53:37.650 align:middle line:90%
That will really you'll be mad.

00:53:37.650 --> 00:53:41.920 align:middle line:84%
The Kilian-Micali
protocol that we built up,

00:53:41.920 --> 00:53:45.560 align:middle line:84%
ah, ah, ah, even
that's not secure.

00:53:45.560 --> 00:53:48.250 align:middle line:84%
The Fiat-Shamir
will be secure on n.

00:53:48.250 --> 00:53:53.870 align:middle line:84%
But this, let me actually put
an asterisk there, it's what--

00:53:53.870 --> 00:53:55.450 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let me tell
you what's known

00:53:55.450 --> 00:53:58.240 align:middle line:84%
about the insecurity of
the Fiat-Shamir paradigm

00:53:58.240 --> 00:53:59.450 align:middle line:90%
for this protocol.

00:53:59.450 --> 00:54:02.750 align:middle line:84%
What is known is there
are some contrived--

00:54:02.750 --> 00:54:05.920 align:middle line:84%
so the Kilian-Micali
protocol says,

00:54:05.920 --> 00:54:10.840 align:middle line:84%
take any hash function
that's collision-resistant

00:54:10.840 --> 00:54:18.190 align:middle line:84%
and take any PCP,
we get soundness.

00:54:18.190 --> 00:54:24.790 align:middle line:84%
There are examples of specific
hash functions or specific PCPs

00:54:24.790 --> 00:54:26.740 align:middle line:84%
that are sound, of
course, because it's--

00:54:26.740 --> 00:54:28.990 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there are examples
of collision-resistant hash

00:54:28.990 --> 00:54:32.450 align:middle line:84%
functions and PCPs such
that this, of course,

00:54:32.450 --> 00:54:35.200 align:middle line:84%
is sound-- it's sound for any
collision hash function PCP,

00:54:35.200 --> 00:54:38.470 align:middle line:84%
but such that no
matter which hash

00:54:38.470 --> 00:54:40.910 align:middle line:84%
function you use
for the Fiat-Shamir,

00:54:40.910 --> 00:54:44.780 align:middle line:84%
this will be
insecure, not sound.

00:54:44.780 --> 00:54:45.835 align:middle line:90%
No matter what you use.

00:54:45.835 --> 00:54:50.600 align:middle line:90%


00:54:50.600 --> 00:54:52.012 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:54:52.012 --> 00:54:54.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Could you do a little
more in detail what breaks?

00:54:54.470 --> 00:54:55.303 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Yeah.

00:54:55.303 --> 00:54:57.930 align:middle line:84%
I'll say a little bit
what breaks in a second.

00:54:57.930 --> 00:54:58.430 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:54:58.430 --> 00:55:00.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
or it's natural?

00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:01.170 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK, good.

00:55:01.170 --> 00:55:01.830 align:middle line:90%
Same question.

00:55:01.830 --> 00:55:06.680 align:middle line:84%
So the example for
why-- so why it breaks?

00:55:06.680 --> 00:55:10.110 align:middle line:84%
The example for why it breaks
is very contrived actually.

00:55:10.110 --> 00:55:14.910 align:middle line:84%
So that's why, is the message
here, don't use Fiat-Shamir.

00:55:14.910 --> 00:55:15.900 align:middle line:90%
We have a break.

00:55:15.900 --> 00:55:17.270 align:middle line:90%
No, that's not the message.

00:55:17.270 --> 00:55:18.950 align:middle line:84%
You should use Fiat-Shamir
because, in practice, it

00:55:18.950 --> 00:55:19.800 align:middle line:90%
works very well.

00:55:19.800 --> 00:55:22.820 align:middle line:84%
I don't have a single instance
that people use Fiat-Shamir

00:55:22.820 --> 00:55:24.590 align:middle line:90%
in practice and it broke.

00:55:24.590 --> 00:55:26.520 align:middle line:90%
So it's a great paradigm.

00:55:26.520 --> 00:55:28.100 align:middle line:90%
Use it.

00:55:28.100 --> 00:55:30.780 align:middle line:84%
Can we come up with a
general proof of security?

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:34.070 align:middle line:84%
No, because we have
counterexamples.

00:55:34.070 --> 00:55:36.300 align:middle line:90%
So what did we learn?

00:55:36.300 --> 00:55:38.900 align:middle line:90%
So maybe I'll start by saying--

00:55:38.900 --> 00:55:42.050 align:middle line:84%
I'll say very, very high
level, not in detail,

00:55:42.050 --> 00:55:45.170 align:middle line:90%
but about the counterexamples.

00:55:45.170 --> 00:55:47.030 align:middle line:84%
How can we build
counterexamples?

00:55:47.030 --> 00:55:49.470 align:middle line:84%
And as opposed to going to
the Kilian-Micali protocol,

00:55:49.470 --> 00:55:52.280 align:middle line:84%
because there you need to work
a little harder to fit it in--

00:55:52.280 --> 00:55:54.480 align:middle line:84%
I'll say a word about how
you fit it in there too.

00:55:54.480 --> 00:55:55.950 align:middle line:90%
But the idea is the following.

00:55:55.950 --> 00:56:00.240 align:middle line:84%
Let me try to give
you a proof that is--

00:56:00.240 --> 00:56:02.880 align:middle line:84%
or an interactive
protocol that is sound.

00:56:02.880 --> 00:56:06.470 align:middle line:90%
But when you convert it to--

00:56:06.470 --> 00:56:09.440 align:middle line:84%
when you apply Fiat-Shamir,
soundness breaks.

00:56:09.440 --> 00:56:12.980 align:middle line:90%
So the idea is the following.

00:56:12.980 --> 00:56:15.120 align:middle line:84%
It can be a very
contrived protocol.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:24.470 align:middle line:84%
So what I want to do is
construct a contrived protocol

00:56:24.470 --> 00:56:26.660 align:middle line:90%
that is sound.

00:56:26.660 --> 00:56:28.400 align:middle line:84%
But when you apply
Fiat-Shamir, no matter

00:56:28.400 --> 00:56:31.880 align:middle line:84%
which hash function
you use, it will break.

00:56:31.880 --> 00:56:35.550 align:middle line:84%
And intuitively, the contrived
protocol is the following.

00:56:35.550 --> 00:56:40.670 align:middle line:84%
Take any sound protocol,
for example, Kilian-Micali.

00:56:40.670 --> 00:56:43.580 align:middle line:84%
Whatever-- or GKR whatever
sound protocol you like.

00:56:43.580 --> 00:56:44.990 align:middle line:90%
And I'm going to change it--

00:56:44.990 --> 00:56:47.780 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to tweak a little bit
that the soundness will still

00:56:47.780 --> 00:56:48.300 align:middle line:90%
remain.

00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:53.960 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to tweak it so that
the soundness will still hold.

00:56:53.960 --> 00:56:56.520 align:middle line:84%
But on the tweaked protocol,
when you apply Fiat-Shamir,

00:56:56.520 --> 00:56:58.920 align:middle line:90%
it will fail.

00:56:58.920 --> 00:57:02.510 align:middle line:84%
So start with PV, for example,
GKR, for example, Kilian-Micali,

00:57:02.510 --> 00:57:04.040 align:middle line:90%
whatever you like.

00:57:04.040 --> 00:57:07.460 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to
tweak it a little bit.

00:57:07.460 --> 00:57:08.970 align:middle line:90%
How am I going to tweak it?

00:57:08.970 --> 00:57:12.590 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to say, tell the
verifier, you know what?

00:57:12.590 --> 00:57:15.710 align:middle line:84%
Accept, if you
accept it before, but

00:57:15.710 --> 00:57:17.700 align:middle line:84%
accept also if something
very weird happens.

00:57:17.700 --> 00:57:21.210 align:middle line:84%
But don't worry, it
will never happen.

00:57:21.210 --> 00:57:22.830 align:middle line:84%
When else are you
going to accept?

00:57:22.830 --> 00:57:28.100 align:middle line:84%
So remember the verifier, let's
say, he sends a random beta.

00:57:28.100 --> 00:57:33.510 align:middle line:84%
If the prover, let's say, ahead
of time, guessed your beta,

00:57:33.510 --> 00:57:35.970 align:middle line:84%
he told you, I know how you're
going to compute your beta.

00:57:35.970 --> 00:57:41.430 align:middle line:84%
So if the prover,
ahead of time, send you

00:57:41.430 --> 00:57:44.250 align:middle line:90%
a message that will tell you--

00:57:44.250 --> 00:57:51.910 align:middle line:84%
say, I can predict beta, and
will predict beta ahead of time,

00:57:51.910 --> 00:57:54.668 align:middle line:84%
then you accept him
because this is random.

00:57:54.668 --> 00:57:56.710 align:middle line:84%
What's the probability
that you can pick my beta?

00:57:56.710 --> 00:57:59.920 align:middle line:84%
If beta is random
0, 1 to the lambda,

00:57:59.920 --> 00:58:01.750 align:middle line:84%
there's no way you
can predict it.

00:58:01.750 --> 00:58:04.170 align:middle line:84%
So if by any chance you
predict I'm going to--

00:58:04.170 --> 00:58:07.242 align:middle line:84%
you won the lottery,
I'll accept you.

00:58:07.242 --> 00:58:08.950 align:middle line:84%
I don't care if x in
the language or not.

00:58:08.950 --> 00:58:12.300 align:middle line:84%
It only adds a negligible
probability of--

00:58:12.300 --> 00:58:15.960 align:middle line:84%
because the probability that you
could predict beta is 1 over 2

00:58:15.960 --> 00:58:16.550 align:middle line:90%
to the beta.

00:58:16.550 --> 00:58:20.550 align:middle line:90%


00:58:20.550 --> 00:58:23.830 align:middle line:84%
Now, what happens in the
Fiat-Shamir paradigm?

00:58:23.830 --> 00:58:26.505 align:middle line:84%
Oh, I can tell you, oh, I
know, beta, I can predict it.

00:58:26.505 --> 00:58:28.630 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to tell you
exactly how I predict it.

00:58:28.630 --> 00:58:33.290 align:middle line:84%
Beta is going to be equal
hash key and this message.

00:58:33.290 --> 00:58:37.650 align:middle line:90%


00:58:37.650 --> 00:58:38.740 align:middle line:90%
That's the idea.

00:58:38.740 --> 00:58:43.000 align:middle line:84%
Now, to implement this
idea, it's harder.

00:58:43.000 --> 00:58:43.930 align:middle line:90%
It requires some work.

00:58:43.930 --> 00:58:47.400 align:middle line:84%
I'll tell you why this idea
is not trivially implemented,

00:58:47.400 --> 00:58:55.470 align:middle line:84%
because when we do Fiat-Shamir,
we say we start with a protocol.

00:58:55.470 --> 00:58:57.510 align:middle line:84%
And now the question
is, does there

00:58:57.510 --> 00:59:02.520 align:middle line:84%
exist a hash of Fiat-Shamir hash
family for which it's secure?

00:59:02.520 --> 00:59:06.880 align:middle line:84%
Now, we saw with the protocol,
this protocol can say,

00:59:06.880 --> 00:59:10.650 align:middle line:84%
OK, alpha is hash
key, for example.

00:59:10.650 --> 00:59:14.670 align:middle line:84%
And then you accept, if
you originally accepted,

00:59:14.670 --> 00:59:22.500 align:middle line:84%
or if eval of hash key of
alpha, which is also hash key,

00:59:22.500 --> 00:59:23.488 align:middle line:90%
is equal to beta.

00:59:23.488 --> 00:59:25.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, this will not happen,
but in the Fiat-Shamir

00:59:25.530 --> 00:59:26.500 align:middle line:90%
it will happen.

00:59:26.500 --> 00:59:28.500 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to just send
the Fiat-Shamir hash key.

00:59:28.500 --> 00:59:30.210 align:middle line:90%
That's what I do.

00:59:30.210 --> 00:59:34.780 align:middle line:84%
But then someone will say,
OK, so you give me a protocol.

00:59:34.780 --> 00:59:36.840 align:middle line:84%
What is the length
of your alpha?

00:59:36.840 --> 00:59:38.320 align:middle line:90%
It's lambda bits.

00:59:38.320 --> 00:59:42.220 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to use a hash function
that the hash key is longer.

00:59:42.220 --> 00:59:44.730 align:middle line:84%
And now you can't send
it because here I'm like,

00:59:44.730 --> 00:59:47.550 align:middle line:90%
I'll send the hash key.

00:59:47.550 --> 00:59:51.160 align:middle line:84%
So then you need to use a hash
to squish this hash key in.

00:59:51.160 --> 00:59:53.250 align:middle line:90%
It's more complicated.

00:59:53.250 --> 00:59:59.070 align:middle line:84%
But the basic idea
is, this does not

00:59:59.070 --> 01:00:05.160 align:middle line:84%
work if somehow the verifier
accepts you if you convinced him

01:00:05.160 --> 01:00:07.750 align:middle line:90%
that you can predict his beta.

01:00:07.750 --> 01:00:16.020 align:middle line:84%
That's really-- and so we can
construct contrived protocols

01:00:16.020 --> 01:00:17.200 align:middle line:90%
that achieve this.

01:00:17.200 --> 01:00:20.640 align:middle line:84%
Or we can construct a very
contrived hash function

01:00:20.640 --> 01:00:23.597 align:middle line:84%
that do this weird game
inside the hash function,

01:00:23.597 --> 01:00:25.680 align:middle line:84%
like the hash function
becomes trivial if you feed

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:28.170 align:middle line:90%
it something that kind of--

01:00:28.170 --> 01:00:32.960 align:middle line:84%
so it requires more work,
but it's all very contrived.

01:00:32.960 --> 01:00:35.320 align:middle line:84%
So I hope I convinced you
by-- with these examples,

01:00:35.320 --> 01:00:37.730 align:middle line:84%
the counterexamples
are so contrived.

01:00:37.730 --> 01:00:41.470 align:middle line:84%
So when you say, oh, we found
that Fiat-Shamir is not secure,

01:00:41.470 --> 01:00:45.860 align:middle line:84%
and, you show this example, the
reaction should be, come on.

01:00:45.860 --> 01:00:48.400 align:middle line:90%
Nobody uses these protocols.

01:00:48.400 --> 01:00:52.970 align:middle line:84%
But what it does tell us, you
can't apply Fiat-Shamir blind.

01:00:52.970 --> 01:00:55.360 align:middle line:84%
What it does tell
us is, for all of us

01:00:55.360 --> 01:00:58.460 align:middle line:84%
who are obsessed with trying
to prove the paradigm,

01:00:58.460 --> 01:01:02.560 align:middle line:84%
there's no general proof
because we have counterexamples.

01:01:02.560 --> 01:01:04.870 align:middle line:90%
At least that.

01:01:04.870 --> 01:01:05.380 align:middle line:90%
Question?

01:01:05.380 --> 01:01:08.380 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you need to
start with the protocol that's

01:01:08.380 --> 01:01:09.285 align:middle line:90%
negligibly sound?

01:01:09.285 --> 01:01:10.160 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Great.

01:01:10.160 --> 01:01:11.860 align:middle line:90%
Great question.

01:01:11.860 --> 01:01:13.765 align:middle line:90%
That's my next one.

01:01:13.765 --> 01:01:14.265 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:01:14.265 --> 01:01:16.810 align:middle line:90%


01:01:16.810 --> 01:01:20.240 align:middle line:84%
The answer is yes, but
I'll talk about that next.

01:01:20.240 --> 01:01:22.240 align:middle line:84%
The question was about
negligible soundness,

01:01:22.240 --> 01:01:24.020 align:middle line:90%
but we'll get to that next.

01:01:24.020 --> 01:01:29.020 align:middle line:90%
So any questions though before--

01:01:29.020 --> 01:01:29.780 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:01:29.780 --> 01:01:33.890 align:middle line:84%
So but, still, people use
the Fiat-Shamir paradigm.

01:01:33.890 --> 01:01:37.870 align:middle line:84%
And there's a question
of is it sound?

01:01:37.870 --> 01:01:40.930 align:middle line:84%
For natural protocols,
is it sound?

01:01:40.930 --> 01:01:43.960 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is, actually,
it would have been nice

01:01:43.960 --> 01:01:46.030 align:middle line:84%
if we can come up
with nice protocols

01:01:46.030 --> 01:01:49.910 align:middle line:84%
and say, at least a large class
of protocols, and say, yeah,

01:01:49.910 --> 01:01:52.580 align:middle line:84%
if you use this hash function,
this specific hash function,

01:01:52.580 --> 01:01:54.100 align:middle line:84%
which we know is
collision-resistant

01:01:54.100 --> 01:01:56.450 align:middle line:84%
under discrete log or under
some other assumption,

01:01:56.450 --> 01:01:57.635 align:middle line:90%
then your scheme is sound.

01:01:57.635 --> 01:01:58.635 align:middle line:90%
That would be very nice.

01:01:58.635 --> 01:02:01.360 align:middle line:90%


01:02:01.360 --> 01:02:04.540 align:middle line:84%
We're still not there yet,
but we made a lot of progress

01:02:04.540 --> 01:02:05.410 align:middle line:90%
in recent years.

01:02:05.410 --> 01:02:07.160 align:middle line:84%
And I want to tell you
about the progress.

01:02:07.160 --> 01:02:15.790 align:middle line:84%
But before that, the
first kind of way

01:02:15.790 --> 01:02:19.150 align:middle line:84%
we tried to analyze-- so the
Fiat-Shamir gained popularity

01:02:19.150 --> 01:02:20.300 align:middle line:90%
very quickly.

01:02:20.300 --> 01:02:24.310 align:middle line:84%
And because it's so
simple and so efficient,

01:02:24.310 --> 01:02:26.390 align:middle line:90%
it became very, very popular.

01:02:26.390 --> 01:02:30.185 align:middle line:84%
And people really wanted
to understand its security.

01:02:30.185 --> 01:02:30.685 align:middle line:90%
Is it sound?

01:02:30.685 --> 01:02:32.560 align:middle line:90%
Is it not sound?

01:02:32.560 --> 01:02:36.880 align:middle line:84%
And then in '93,
Bellare and Rogaway

01:02:36.880 --> 01:02:40.755 align:middle line:84%
introduced an ideal model
called the Random Oracle Model.

01:02:40.755 --> 01:02:55.150 align:middle line:90%


01:02:55.150 --> 01:02:58.870 align:middle line:84%
And this model
said, you know what?

01:02:58.870 --> 01:03:02.590 align:middle line:84%
By the way, this was before
we had any counterexamples.

01:03:02.590 --> 01:03:06.250 align:middle line:84%
This model was introduced
by Bellare and Rogaway

01:03:06.250 --> 01:03:11.590 align:middle line:90%
in, I believe, '93.

01:03:11.590 --> 01:03:13.660 align:middle line:84%
This was before we knew
any counterexamples

01:03:13.660 --> 01:03:15.020 align:middle line:90%
for the paradigm.

01:03:15.020 --> 01:03:18.910 align:middle line:84%
And they-- I mean,
except for some,

01:03:18.910 --> 01:03:22.520 align:middle line:84%
which goes to your question,
Leo, but I'm going to get to it.

01:03:22.520 --> 01:03:25.780 align:middle line:84%
And they wanted to ask,
can we prove security

01:03:25.780 --> 01:03:30.360 align:middle line:90%
of the Fiat-Shamir--

01:03:30.360 --> 01:03:32.070 align:middle line:84%
what do we need about
the hash function?

01:03:32.070 --> 01:03:34.153 align:middle line:84%
What properties do we need
about the hash function

01:03:34.153 --> 01:03:34.950 align:middle line:90%
to prove security?

01:03:34.950 --> 01:03:37.230 align:middle line:84%
And the first property
they said, you know what?

01:03:37.230 --> 01:03:41.330 align:middle line:84%
What if the hash function
was completely random?

01:03:41.330 --> 01:03:43.040 align:middle line:84%
So when you use
Fiat-Shamir, we use

01:03:43.040 --> 01:03:45.665 align:middle line:84%
a specific-- there's a circuit
that computes the hash function.

01:03:45.665 --> 01:03:48.080 align:middle line:84%
It's like a Turing machine,
a polynomial time Turing

01:03:48.080 --> 01:03:49.580 align:middle line:84%
machine or a
polynomial size circuit

01:03:49.580 --> 01:03:51.140 align:middle line:90%
takes a hash key and, OK.

01:03:51.140 --> 01:03:52.140 align:middle line:90%
They say, you know what?

01:03:52.140 --> 01:03:55.490 align:middle line:84%
What if the hash function
is completely random?

01:03:55.490 --> 01:04:01.160 align:middle line:84%
So the Random Oracle
Model says, suppose

01:04:01.160 --> 01:04:14.870 align:middle line:84%
we apply Fiat-Shamir paradigm
with truly random function

01:04:14.870 --> 01:04:18.630 align:middle line:84%
H. So this is completely,
completely random.

01:04:18.630 --> 01:04:20.660 align:middle line:84%
So what do I mean by
completely random?

01:04:20.660 --> 01:04:30.800 align:middle line:84%
So now, I guess, let me,
instead of choosing a function

01:04:30.800 --> 01:04:37.080 align:middle line:84%
from a family, we don't choose
a function, and we just say,

01:04:37.080 --> 01:04:39.450 align:middle line:84%
suppose they have
a truly random--

01:04:39.450 --> 01:04:41.450 align:middle line:84%
they have access to
a-- both of them--

01:04:41.450 --> 01:04:44.510 align:middle line:84%
to a truly, truly
random function.

01:04:44.510 --> 01:04:45.780 align:middle line:90%
And they can ask.

01:04:45.780 --> 01:04:49.500 align:middle line:84%
So when the prover
computes alpha,

01:04:49.500 --> 01:04:51.800 align:middle line:84%
he asks-- he has Oracle
access to this function.

01:04:51.800 --> 01:04:54.320 align:middle line:84%
And he tells the
function, give me

01:04:54.320 --> 01:04:58.950 align:middle line:84%
the function applied to x and
alpha, and he gets back beta.

01:04:58.950 --> 01:05:03.300 align:middle line:84%
And every time this hash,
this Oracle, he gets a query,

01:05:03.300 --> 01:05:05.060 align:middle line:84%
he chooses a
completely random-- so

01:05:05.060 --> 01:05:09.080 align:middle line:84%
think of this as a database
of all poly of size 2

01:05:09.080 --> 01:05:12.740 align:middle line:84%
to the lambda of all the--
just random, random elements.

01:05:12.740 --> 01:05:16.100 align:middle line:84%
So really, really
truly random function.

01:05:16.100 --> 01:05:19.950 align:middle line:84%
So now the question was,
OK, if this is truly random,

01:05:19.950 --> 01:05:21.380 align:middle line:90%
now can we get security?

01:05:21.380 --> 01:05:24.000 align:middle line:90%
At least, that's the minimum.

01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:27.530 align:middle line:84%
And moreover, it seemed like the
answer should be yes because,

01:05:27.530 --> 01:05:31.530 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, what
was their intuition?

01:05:31.530 --> 01:05:33.860 align:middle line:84%
The intuition is,
what does it matter

01:05:33.860 --> 01:05:38.180 align:middle line:84%
if you're talking to a verifier
that gives you random queries

01:05:38.180 --> 01:05:41.130 align:middle line:84%
or you're talking to,
in terms of the prover.

01:05:41.130 --> 01:05:42.810 align:middle line:84%
So the prover is
trying to cheat.

01:05:42.810 --> 01:05:44.900 align:middle line:84%
What does it matter if
he's talking to a verifier

01:05:44.900 --> 01:05:47.540 align:middle line:84%
to give him random
betas, or he's

01:05:47.540 --> 01:05:51.270 align:middle line:84%
talking to a random function
that gives him random betas?

01:05:51.270 --> 01:05:52.710 align:middle line:90%
Both of them give random betas.

01:05:52.710 --> 01:05:54.350 align:middle line:90%
What's the difference?

01:05:54.350 --> 01:05:58.500 align:middle line:84%
So it should at least be secure
in the Random Oracle Model.

01:05:58.500 --> 01:06:01.910 align:middle line:90%
Let's first establish that.

01:06:01.910 --> 01:06:04.055 align:middle line:84%
So is it secure in the
Random Oracle Model.

01:06:04.055 --> 01:06:04.555 align:middle line:90%
No.

01:06:04.555 --> 01:06:07.310 align:middle line:90%


01:06:07.310 --> 01:06:09.622 align:middle line:90%
And this goes to Leo's question.

01:06:09.622 --> 01:06:12.080 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going to tell you why
it's not secure in the Random

01:06:12.080 --> 01:06:12.990 align:middle line:90%
Oracle Model.

01:06:12.990 --> 01:06:20.270 align:middle line:84%
And actually, let me take a
little detour and tell you--

01:06:20.270 --> 01:06:23.030 align:middle line:84%
I'll show why it's not
secure in the Random Oracle

01:06:23.030 --> 01:06:26.220 align:middle line:90%
Model via a little protocol.

01:06:26.220 --> 01:06:29.720 align:middle line:84%
This protocol actually is going
to be useful because, actually,

01:06:29.720 --> 01:06:31.880 align:middle line:84%
later, we will show
that the Fiat-Shamir

01:06:31.880 --> 01:06:33.510 align:middle line:90%
is sound for this protocol.

01:06:33.510 --> 01:06:34.470 align:middle line:90%
It's kind of weird.

01:06:34.470 --> 01:06:37.230 align:middle line:84%
But let's-- this
protocol, by the way,

01:06:37.230 --> 01:06:39.410 align:middle line:90%
is very important protocol.

01:06:39.410 --> 01:06:44.580 align:middle line:84%
So let me quickly do--
let me do a little detour.

01:06:44.580 --> 01:06:46.530 align:middle line:84%
I think it's a really
interesting detour.

01:06:46.530 --> 01:06:48.800 align:middle line:90%
So I hope you enjoy it.

01:06:48.800 --> 01:06:52.260 align:middle line:84%
It takes us a little bit into
the land of cryptography,

01:06:52.260 --> 01:06:53.440 align:middle line:90%
but not too bad--

01:06:53.440 --> 01:06:56.420 align:middle line:90%


01:06:56.420 --> 01:06:58.410 align:middle line:84%
a little further
than we went so far.

01:06:58.410 --> 01:07:02.810 align:middle line:84%
So far we just talked
about hash functions.

01:07:02.810 --> 01:07:08.240 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going
to say now is going

01:07:08.240 --> 01:07:10.310 align:middle line:84%
to be a little bit
of a high level.

01:07:10.310 --> 01:07:12.950 align:middle line:90%


01:07:12.950 --> 01:07:18.470 align:middle line:84%
So a protocol that
I want to talk about

01:07:18.470 --> 01:07:20.940 align:middle line:84%
is actually a zero
knowledge protocol.

01:07:20.940 --> 01:07:25.170 align:middle line:84%
So, so far, we just talked about
verification, verification,

01:07:25.170 --> 01:07:27.720 align:middle line:90%
succinctness, and efficiency.

01:07:27.720 --> 01:07:31.140 align:middle line:84%
But, as I mentioned,
interactive protocols

01:07:31.140 --> 01:07:34.170 align:middle line:84%
were actually invented for
the sake of zero knowledge.

01:07:34.170 --> 01:07:35.530 align:middle line:90%
So what was the goal?

01:07:35.530 --> 01:07:39.058 align:middle line:84%
The goal was, forget
about succinctness now.

01:07:39.058 --> 01:07:40.600 align:middle line:84%
It was not at all
about succinctness.

01:07:40.600 --> 01:07:44.980 align:middle line:84%
All we wanted is a prover
that has, let's say,

01:07:44.980 --> 01:07:46.580 align:middle line:90%
a witness for some--

01:07:46.580 --> 01:07:48.030 align:middle line:90%
there's some language.

01:07:48.030 --> 01:07:50.160 align:middle line:84%
And let's say,
the language we're

01:07:50.160 --> 01:07:53.230 align:middle line:84%
going to be interested for
today, just as an example,

01:07:53.230 --> 01:07:55.640 align:middle line:84%
is the language of
Hamiltonian cycle.

01:07:55.640 --> 01:07:58.800 align:middle line:90%


01:07:58.800 --> 01:08:00.070 align:middle line:90%
That's just an example.

01:08:00.070 --> 01:08:03.630 align:middle line:84%
So this language
consists of graphs.

01:08:03.630 --> 01:08:06.850 align:middle line:84%
So this is the language
that consists of all graphs.

01:08:06.850 --> 01:08:10.650 align:middle line:84%
Graph is just consists
of nodes and edges

01:08:10.650 --> 01:08:14.970 align:middle line:84%
such that this graph has
a Hamiltonian cycle such

01:08:14.970 --> 01:08:23.439 align:middle line:90%
that G has a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:08:23.439 --> 01:08:29.640 align:middle line:84%
What this means is
just, you can just--

01:08:29.640 --> 01:08:34.450 align:middle line:84%
there's a kind of a
cycle of all the nodes.

01:08:34.450 --> 01:08:36.640 align:middle line:84%
So it means that there's
a cycle of all the nodes.

01:08:36.640 --> 01:08:43.920 align:middle line:84%
So let's say you start with node
i1, i2, up to i n, and then go--

01:08:43.920 --> 01:08:45.540 align:middle line:84%
up to i n so that
there's an edge

01:08:45.540 --> 01:08:50.220 align:middle line:84%
here, edge, edge, edge, edge,
edge, and edge all the way back.

01:08:50.220 --> 01:08:57.029 align:middle line:84%
That means, if you can-- if
there is some such a setting

01:08:57.029 --> 01:09:00.149 align:middle line:84%
of the nodes for which
you can set them in a way

01:09:00.149 --> 01:09:02.680 align:middle line:84%
that there's edges
that complete a cycle,

01:09:02.680 --> 01:09:05.970 align:middle line:84%
then we say the graph
has a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:09:05.970 --> 01:09:09.100 align:middle line:90%
This language is NP complete.

01:09:09.100 --> 01:09:13.090 align:middle line:84%
Now, so now going back, what
is the zero knowledge proof?

01:09:13.090 --> 01:09:14.760 align:middle line:84%
A zero knowledge
proof is a way I

01:09:14.760 --> 01:09:19.490 align:middle line:84%
want to convince you that a
graph G has a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:09:19.490 --> 01:09:21.240 align:middle line:84%
Now, I can give you
the Hamiltonian cycle.

01:09:21.240 --> 01:09:22.170 align:middle line:90%
I can give you-- see?

01:09:22.170 --> 01:09:23.170 align:middle line:90%
Here's the nodes.

01:09:23.170 --> 01:09:24.990 align:middle line:84%
You can look, there's
a cycle there.

01:09:24.990 --> 01:09:27.279 align:middle line:84%
But I don't want to give
you any information.

01:09:27.279 --> 01:09:29.770 align:middle line:84%
I don't want you
to learn anything.

01:09:29.770 --> 01:09:31.835 align:middle line:84%
I don't want you to find
the Hamiltonian cycle.

01:09:31.835 --> 01:09:33.460 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to reveal
any information,

01:09:33.460 --> 01:09:36.005 align:middle line:84%
but I want to convince you that
it has a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:09:36.005 --> 01:09:39.270 align:middle line:90%


01:09:39.270 --> 01:09:41.189 align:middle line:90%
How do I do that?

01:09:41.189 --> 01:09:45.010 align:middle line:84%
So this kind of-- so
there's a celebrated result.

01:09:45.010 --> 01:09:46.800 align:middle line:84%
It started with
Goldwasser, Micali,

01:09:46.800 --> 01:09:51.300 align:middle line:84%
and Markov in the mid '80s and
where they defined this notion

01:09:51.300 --> 01:09:54.340 align:middle line:84%
of zero knowledge and
constructed some protocols.

01:09:54.340 --> 01:10:00.300 align:middle line:84%
But later, very shortly
later, by Goldreich, Micali,

01:10:00.300 --> 01:10:04.740 align:middle line:84%
and Wigderson showed that,
actually, any PNP language

01:10:04.740 --> 01:10:07.750 align:middle line:90%
has a zero knowledge proof.

01:10:07.750 --> 01:10:10.140 align:middle line:84%
Let me give you one
specific zero knowledge

01:10:10.140 --> 01:10:13.620 align:middle line:84%
proof for the Hamiltonian
cycle language.

01:10:13.620 --> 01:10:16.080 align:middle line:90%
And this is due to Blum.

01:10:16.080 --> 01:10:18.260 align:middle line:90%
So I'll show you the proof.

01:10:18.260 --> 01:10:19.810 align:middle line:90%
It's very, very nice.

01:10:19.810 --> 01:10:23.920 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to be useful for
us to show why the Random Oracle

01:10:23.920 --> 01:10:26.218 align:middle line:84%
Model fails, and then
also to show the security

01:10:26.218 --> 01:10:27.010 align:middle line:90%
of the Fiat-Shamir.

01:10:27.010 --> 01:10:30.520 align:middle line:84%
So this is actually-- we'll
get back to this protocol.

01:10:30.520 --> 01:10:33.460 align:middle line:90%
So what is this protocol?

01:10:33.460 --> 01:10:35.740 align:middle line:90%
There's a prover and a verifier.

01:10:35.740 --> 01:10:37.690 align:middle line:90%
The idea is the following.

01:10:37.690 --> 01:10:44.650 align:middle line:84%
The prover will-- he knows--
he will first-- he has a G.

01:10:44.650 --> 01:10:47.530 align:middle line:84%
The first thing he's
going to permute--

01:10:47.530 --> 01:10:49.910 align:middle line:90%
completely permute the nodes.

01:10:49.910 --> 01:10:52.310 align:middle line:84%
So he's going to choose
a random permutation pi.

01:10:52.310 --> 01:10:55.150 align:middle line:84%
Let's say n is
the number denoted

01:10:55.150 --> 01:10:58.960 align:middle line:84%
by n, the number
of vertices in G.

01:10:58.960 --> 01:11:06.100 align:middle line:84%
He's going to choose a random
permutation of the nodes.

01:11:06.100 --> 01:11:08.780 align:middle line:84%
Now he has the permuted
graph in his head.

01:11:08.780 --> 01:11:12.670 align:middle line:90%
So he has pi of G in his head.

01:11:12.670 --> 01:11:16.030 align:middle line:84%
Now this, it has, this pi
of G has a Hamiltonian cycle

01:11:16.030 --> 01:11:18.890 align:middle line:84%
because G has and I
just renamed the nodes.

01:11:18.890 --> 01:11:20.800 align:middle line:90%
Now I'm going to--

01:11:20.800 --> 01:11:24.860 align:middle line:84%
I want to give you just
this Hamiltonian cycle.

01:11:24.860 --> 01:11:27.440 align:middle line:84%
So I want to give you the nodes
here that form a Hamiltonian.

01:11:27.440 --> 01:11:29.450 align:middle line:90%
So I want to give you--

01:11:29.450 --> 01:11:31.660 align:middle line:84%
I would like to give
you the nodes that

01:11:31.660 --> 01:11:34.270 align:middle line:84%
form the Hamiltonian, just
the Hamiltonian cycle.

01:11:34.270 --> 01:11:37.900 align:middle line:84%
But I can't give you, that
will reveal information.

01:11:37.900 --> 01:11:39.650 align:middle line:90%
So I don't want to do that.

01:11:39.650 --> 01:11:45.425 align:middle line:84%
Instead, I'm going to give
it to you in a safe, hidden.

01:11:45.425 --> 01:11:47.050 align:middle line:84%
This is what's called,
in cryptography,

01:11:47.050 --> 01:11:48.620 align:middle line:90%
we call this commitment scheme.

01:11:48.620 --> 01:11:56.102 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to give you a
commitment of the cycle C.

01:11:56.102 --> 01:12:01.316 align:middle line:84%
So think of this, for all the
possible edges, and grid 0, 0,

01:12:01.316 --> 01:12:03.840 align:middle line:84%
0, 0, only in the cycle
I'm going to put 1.

01:12:03.840 --> 01:12:04.840 align:middle line:90%
That's what I mean here.

01:12:04.840 --> 01:12:06.760 align:middle line:90%
Because, so this is--

01:12:06.760 --> 01:12:12.850 align:middle line:84%
think of it as a
matrix of n by n--

01:12:12.850 --> 01:12:16.690 align:middle line:90%
sorry, yeah, n by n.

01:12:16.690 --> 01:12:20.320 align:middle line:84%
For any possible edge, for
any possible nodes, i and j,

01:12:20.320 --> 01:12:23.710 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to put 0, like
there is no edge, only

01:12:23.710 --> 01:12:26.110 align:middle line:84%
if it's in the cycle I'm
going to put an edge there.

01:12:26.110 --> 01:12:26.890 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:12:26.890 --> 01:12:30.710 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So a cycle is on the
label of the permuted graph.

01:12:30.710 --> 01:12:34.210 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Exactly,
on the permuted graph.

01:12:34.210 --> 01:12:37.510 align:middle line:84%
The reason I permute the graph
is to ensure zero knowledgeness.

01:12:37.510 --> 01:12:39.700 align:middle line:84%
Even though when I
send the commitment,

01:12:39.700 --> 01:12:41.230 align:middle line:90%
really, think of it--

01:12:41.230 --> 01:12:44.240 align:middle line:84%
we're going to later talk about
how to do this commitment.

01:12:44.240 --> 01:12:47.240 align:middle line:84%
But for now, think of
it, there's a safe.

01:12:47.240 --> 01:12:50.150 align:middle line:84%
I'm putting my commitment
inside the safe.

01:12:50.150 --> 01:12:52.640 align:middle line:84%
I leave the key to myself
and I give you this box.

01:12:52.640 --> 01:12:55.550 align:middle line:90%
So you learn nothing.

01:12:55.550 --> 01:12:57.580 align:middle line:84%
So here, really,
you learn nothing.

01:12:57.580 --> 01:13:00.220 align:middle line:84%
But now-- but you're
not convinced either

01:13:00.220 --> 01:13:01.400 align:middle line:90%
because you just got a safe.

01:13:01.400 --> 01:13:02.775 align:middle line:84%
What are you going
to do with it?

01:13:02.775 --> 01:13:05.440 align:middle line:84%
So now here's how I'm going to
convince you that, actually,

01:13:05.440 --> 01:13:10.000 align:middle line:84%
this graph has a Hamiltonian
cycle without leaking

01:13:10.000 --> 01:13:11.770 align:middle line:90%
any information.

01:13:11.770 --> 01:13:15.540 align:middle line:84%
So you, the verifier, you're
going to give me a random bit

01:13:15.540 --> 01:13:23.630 align:middle line:84%
B. This is going to
be random, random bit.

01:13:23.630 --> 01:13:30.900 align:middle line:84%
Now, if B is 0, I'm
just going to open this.

01:13:30.900 --> 01:13:36.860 align:middle line:90%
So if B is 0, if B is 0, open.

01:13:36.860 --> 01:13:38.570 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to open the safe.

01:13:38.570 --> 01:13:40.430 align:middle line:84%
And now you can see
that what's sitting

01:13:40.430 --> 01:13:44.180 align:middle line:90%
there is a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:13:44.180 --> 01:13:46.760 align:middle line:90%
Now, what did you learn?

01:13:46.760 --> 01:13:50.250 align:middle line:84%
Well, because I permuted,
it's just a random cycle.

01:13:50.250 --> 01:13:52.580 align:middle line:84%
Actually, it has nothing
to do with the graph.

01:13:52.580 --> 01:13:57.920 align:middle line:84%
I can even first just choose
a random cycle, commit to it,

01:13:57.920 --> 01:14:01.940 align:middle line:84%
and then post choose
this permutation

01:14:01.940 --> 01:14:05.340 align:middle line:90%
to align with this cycle.

01:14:05.340 --> 01:14:08.970 align:middle line:84%
So really, because I chose
a random permutation,

01:14:08.970 --> 01:14:11.182 align:middle line:90%
it's just a random cycle.

01:14:11.182 --> 01:14:12.390 align:middle line:90%
So you didn't learn anything.

01:14:12.390 --> 01:14:13.790 align:middle line:90%
It's just a random cycle.

01:14:13.790 --> 01:14:16.580 align:middle line:84%
You and yourself can put
a random cycle in a safe.

01:14:16.580 --> 01:14:20.480 align:middle line:90%
So really, no information.

01:14:20.480 --> 01:14:23.840 align:middle line:90%
What if B equals 1?

01:14:23.840 --> 01:14:29.180 align:middle line:84%
If B equals 1, I want to--
first I want to give you pi.

01:14:29.180 --> 01:14:30.240 align:middle line:90%
So I give you pi.

01:14:30.240 --> 01:14:32.210 align:middle line:90%
But now I can't open this.

01:14:32.210 --> 01:14:34.790 align:middle line:84%
Because now, if you know
pi, and you see the cycle,

01:14:34.790 --> 01:14:37.490 align:middle line:84%
you know what my
Hamiltonian cycle is.

01:14:37.490 --> 01:14:39.230 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going
to do, I just want

01:14:39.230 --> 01:14:47.450 align:middle line:84%
to convince you that
this cycle only has

01:14:47.450 --> 01:14:50.720 align:middle line:90%
edges that were in this graph.

01:14:50.720 --> 01:14:55.170 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to open
only the non-edges.

01:14:55.170 --> 01:14:57.560 align:middle line:84%
So instead of
opening everything,

01:14:57.560 --> 01:15:09.590 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to open non-edges
in the permuted graph.

01:15:09.590 --> 01:15:13.860 align:middle line:84%
So note, here we
have-- so I have here--

01:15:13.860 --> 01:15:16.770 align:middle line:84%
so there's a question,
how do I open?

01:15:16.770 --> 01:15:22.470 align:middle line:84%
So think of it, I commit
to n squared bits.

01:15:22.470 --> 01:15:26.550 align:middle line:84%
For any possible ij, I have a
bit saying, is it 0 or is it 1?

01:15:26.550 --> 01:15:28.380 align:middle line:84%
Is there an edge or
is it not an edge?

01:15:28.380 --> 01:15:31.940 align:middle line:84%
Think of it as I'm giving
you n squared safes.

01:15:31.940 --> 01:15:35.250 align:middle line:84%
Now, if B is 0, I
open all the safes.

01:15:35.250 --> 01:15:37.610 align:middle line:84%
I open all the safes
and you see, oh, there's

01:15:37.610 --> 01:15:39.620 align:middle line:90%
only-- there's just a cycle.

01:15:39.620 --> 01:15:44.900 align:middle line:84%
There's everything is 0
except for i1 and i2 are--

01:15:44.900 --> 01:15:47.730 align:middle line:84%
there's one, and then
i2, i3, that's it.

01:15:47.730 --> 01:15:49.820 align:middle line:90%
Everything else is 0.

01:15:49.820 --> 01:15:53.250 align:middle line:84%
If you send me a 1, then
I open something else.

01:15:53.250 --> 01:15:54.630 align:middle line:90%
I don't open everything.

01:15:54.630 --> 01:15:55.770 align:middle line:90%
The opposite.

01:15:55.770 --> 01:15:59.660 align:middle line:90%
What I do is, I give you pi.

01:15:59.660 --> 01:16:06.890 align:middle line:84%
And for every non-edge in
pi, I open the non-edge safe

01:16:06.890 --> 01:16:10.020 align:middle line:90%
and I show you, see, there's 0.

01:16:10.020 --> 01:16:14.950 align:middle line:84%
So all the safes I opened
are just going to be 0.

01:16:14.950 --> 01:16:16.450 align:middle line:84%
So you really didn't
learn anything.

01:16:16.450 --> 01:16:17.680 align:middle line:90%
I just opened zeros.

01:16:17.680 --> 01:16:20.070 align:middle line:84%
You can simulate
that in your head.

01:16:20.070 --> 01:16:21.120 align:middle line:90%
What do you learn?

01:16:21.120 --> 01:16:25.530 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, you know
that if you accept me,

01:16:25.530 --> 01:16:28.830 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to open
to just zeros there.

01:16:28.830 --> 01:16:30.700 align:middle line:84%
And the pi doesn't
reveal anything.

01:16:30.700 --> 01:16:34.350 align:middle line:90%
It's just a random permutation.

01:16:34.350 --> 01:16:39.070 align:middle line:84%
So note, you, the verifier,
didn't learn anything.

01:16:39.070 --> 01:16:44.180 align:middle line:84%
If you open to 0, if you send
me 0, I just open random cycle.

01:16:44.180 --> 01:16:45.930 align:middle line:84%
Has nothing to do with
the graph, nothing,

01:16:45.930 --> 01:16:48.450 align:middle line:90%
just random cycle.

01:16:48.450 --> 01:16:50.490 align:middle line:90%
That's not helpful for you.

01:16:50.490 --> 01:16:55.320 align:middle line:84%
If you open 1, I gave you
completely random permutation.

01:16:55.320 --> 01:17:00.960 align:middle line:84%
You know pi G on your own,
and I opened to zeros.

01:17:00.960 --> 01:17:02.800 align:middle line:90%
All the non-edges are open to 0.

01:17:02.800 --> 01:17:05.790 align:middle line:90%
You learn nothing.

01:17:05.790 --> 01:17:12.240 align:middle line:84%
Still, you are somewhat
convinced that this protocol--

01:17:12.240 --> 01:17:14.440 align:middle line:90%
that the graph is Hamiltonian.

01:17:14.440 --> 01:17:17.130 align:middle line:84%
Why are you somewhat
convinced that there's

01:17:17.130 --> 01:17:18.340 align:middle line:90%
a Hamiltonian cycle?

01:17:18.340 --> 01:17:19.990 align:middle line:90%
Why are you somewhat convinced?

01:17:19.990 --> 01:17:23.790 align:middle line:84%
Because if there is no
Hamiltonian cycle in G,

01:17:23.790 --> 01:17:28.050 align:middle line:90%
I cannot open to both 0 and 1.

01:17:28.050 --> 01:17:30.340 align:middle line:84%
Why is it the case
that I cannot open?

01:17:30.340 --> 01:17:32.160 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:17:32.160 --> 01:17:35.670 align:middle line:84%
I'm achieving-- let's
say the graph, is

01:17:35.670 --> 01:17:39.780 align:middle line:90%
there is no Hamiltonian cycle.

01:17:39.780 --> 01:17:44.460 align:middle line:84%
Now, I sent you these
n squared safes.

01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:48.660 align:middle line:84%
Case one, there's indeed only
a Hamiltonian cycle there.

01:17:48.660 --> 01:17:52.050 align:middle line:84%
Indeed, when you open all
these n safes, what you see

01:17:52.050 --> 01:17:55.800 align:middle line:84%
is everything is 0 except
for Hamiltonian cycle.

01:17:55.800 --> 01:17:59.440 align:middle line:84%
If that's not the case,
I cannot open to 0.

01:17:59.440 --> 01:18:03.840 align:middle line:90%
If I open to 0, you catch me.

01:18:03.840 --> 01:18:06.400 align:middle line:84%
Now, suppose I did
succeed in opening to 0.

01:18:06.400 --> 01:18:09.250 align:middle line:84%
Namely, there's just a
Hamiltonian cycle there.

01:18:09.250 --> 01:18:11.280 align:middle line:90%
That's it.

01:18:11.280 --> 01:18:14.640 align:middle line:84%
Then I claim there's
no way I can open to 1.

01:18:14.640 --> 01:18:16.260 align:middle line:90%
Why?

01:18:16.260 --> 01:18:18.660 align:middle line:84%
If there is a
Hamiltonian cycle there,

01:18:18.660 --> 01:18:23.280 align:middle line:84%
I know the Hamiltonian cycle
is not on the non-edges

01:18:23.280 --> 01:18:25.500 align:middle line:90%
because the non-edges are 0.

01:18:25.500 --> 01:18:28.260 align:middle line:84%
If I open on one,
the non-edges are 0.

01:18:28.260 --> 01:18:30.070 align:middle line:84%
So the Hamiltonian
cycle is on the edges.

01:18:30.070 --> 01:18:31.625 align:middle line:84%
But there is no
Hamiltonian cycle.

01:18:31.625 --> 01:18:34.800 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] It can't
be on the edges.

01:18:34.800 --> 01:18:38.700 align:middle line:84%
So either-- I'm achieving
prover-- either I can--

01:18:38.700 --> 01:18:40.750 align:middle line:90%
I may be able to open to 0.

01:18:40.750 --> 01:18:44.250 align:middle line:84%
I may be able to open to 1,
but I can't open to both.

01:18:44.250 --> 01:18:48.600 align:middle line:84%
So with probability
1/2, you'll reject me.

01:18:48.600 --> 01:18:52.233 align:middle line:84%
Half isn't that great,
but it's something.

01:18:52.233 --> 01:18:54.150 align:middle line:84%
So at least now we know,
with probability 1/2,

01:18:54.150 --> 01:18:55.192 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to be rejected.

01:18:55.192 --> 01:18:59.730 align:middle line:90%


01:18:59.730 --> 01:19:01.890 align:middle line:90%
You like the detour?

01:19:01.890 --> 01:19:03.550 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's go back to
the random Oracle.

01:19:03.550 --> 01:19:04.830 align:middle line:90%
But before-- yeah.

01:19:04.830 --> 01:19:07.330 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So do you have to
compute the whole graph, or just

01:19:07.330 --> 01:19:08.860 align:middle line:90%
a cycle?

01:19:08.860 --> 01:19:11.350 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: So what--
no, what I commit to is,

01:19:11.350 --> 01:19:14.980 align:middle line:84%
actually, I have n
squared commitments.

01:19:14.980 --> 01:19:19.600 align:middle line:84%
Everything is 0 except
for a random cycle.

01:19:19.600 --> 01:19:21.440 align:middle line:90%
I do-- I only commit to zeros.

01:19:21.440 --> 01:19:23.840 align:middle line:84%
Why is it important that
I only commit to zeros?

01:19:23.840 --> 01:19:28.580 align:middle line:90%
Because when I open-- ah, OK.

01:19:28.580 --> 01:19:32.200 align:middle line:84%
I want to make sure that
there's a cycle here.

01:19:32.200 --> 01:19:35.240 align:middle line:84%
But when I open to 1, I don't
want to reveal anything.

01:19:35.240 --> 01:19:37.120 align:middle line:84%
And the way I don't
reveal anything

01:19:37.120 --> 01:19:41.570 align:middle line:84%
is I show that all the
non-edges in pi, in pi of g,

01:19:41.570 --> 01:19:45.722 align:middle line:84%
if you open them
here, you'll get a 0.

01:19:45.722 --> 01:19:46.430 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Oh, OK.

01:19:46.430 --> 01:19:47.330 align:middle line:90%
So it's a matrix.

01:19:47.330 --> 01:19:49.930 align:middle line:84%
So it already kind
of has everything--

01:19:49.930 --> 01:19:52.392 align:middle line:84%
with the cycle, like
outlining the cycle.

01:19:52.392 --> 01:19:53.350 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Exactly.

01:19:53.350 --> 01:19:53.920 align:middle line:90%
Exactly.

01:19:53.920 --> 01:19:56.230 align:middle line:90%
What I commit to is a matrix--

01:19:56.230 --> 01:19:57.910 align:middle line:84%
what I commit to
is a matrix that

01:19:57.910 --> 01:20:00.350 align:middle line:84%
only has a Hamiltonian
cycle and that's it.

01:20:00.350 --> 01:20:03.640 align:middle line:90%
Everything else is 0.

01:20:03.640 --> 01:20:04.300 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:20:04.300 --> 01:20:09.250 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So in the second
case, the verifier cannot use

01:20:09.250 --> 01:20:12.880 align:middle line:84%
the permutation to figure
out what edges are not

01:20:12.880 --> 01:20:14.920 align:middle line:84%
in the Hamiltonian cycle
for the original graph.

01:20:14.920 --> 01:20:15.795 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Right.

01:20:15.795 --> 01:20:18.050 align:middle line:84%
So in the B equals 1 case
you're talking about?

01:20:18.050 --> 01:20:18.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:20:18.550 --> 01:20:21.518 align:middle line:84%
In the B equals 1
case, the verifier--

01:20:21.518 --> 01:20:22.310 align:middle line:90%
what does he learn?

01:20:22.310 --> 01:20:23.840 align:middle line:90%
He learns pi, the permutation.

01:20:23.840 --> 01:20:25.960 align:middle line:84%
That's just a
random permutation.

01:20:25.960 --> 01:20:27.970 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm just
telling you, I'm

01:20:27.970 --> 01:20:29.748 align:middle line:84%
reassuring you
that I didn't lie.

01:20:29.748 --> 01:20:31.040 align:middle line:90%
What do I mean by reassure you?

01:20:31.040 --> 01:20:33.887 align:middle line:84%
I open-- everybody, you can
compute pi G on your own.

01:20:33.887 --> 01:20:34.720 align:middle line:90%
That's not a secret.

01:20:34.720 --> 01:20:36.050 align:middle line:90%
G is given, pi is given.

01:20:36.050 --> 01:20:38.150 align:middle line:84%
You can find G. You
see all the non-edges.

01:20:38.150 --> 01:20:41.230 align:middle line:84%
That's not a-- and
all the non-edges,

01:20:41.230 --> 01:20:44.040 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to open only the
non-edges and show you zeros.

01:20:44.040 --> 01:20:46.600 align:middle line:90%


01:20:46.600 --> 01:20:51.130 align:middle line:84%
Because the point is, if I
was honest, what I should do,

01:20:51.130 --> 01:20:54.320 align:middle line:84%
is put ones only on edges,
and not only on edges,

01:20:54.320 --> 01:20:56.410 align:middle line:84%
only on the Hamiltonian
and the edges that

01:20:56.410 --> 01:20:58.480 align:middle line:90%
form a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:20:58.480 --> 01:21:00.320 align:middle line:84%
That's what I should
do if I'm honest.

01:21:00.320 --> 01:21:02.883 align:middle line:84%
And what I show you,
I don't show you

01:21:02.883 --> 01:21:04.550 align:middle line:84%
that because that
will give information.

01:21:04.550 --> 01:21:05.930 align:middle line:90%
But at least what I show you.

01:21:05.930 --> 01:21:09.760 align:middle line:84%
Then all the non-edges
here, I committed to 0.

01:21:09.760 --> 01:21:12.850 align:middle line:84%
And that gives you
no information.

01:21:12.850 --> 01:21:18.260 align:middle line:84%
But if, indeed, the
non-edges are 0,

01:21:18.260 --> 01:21:20.500 align:middle line:84%
and there's a
Hamiltonian cycle, that

01:21:20.500 --> 01:21:23.240 align:middle line:84%
means the Hamiltonian
cycle must be edges.

01:21:23.240 --> 01:21:26.680 align:middle line:84%
And then it means that pi of
G has a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:21:26.680 --> 01:21:27.740 align:middle line:90%
And that can't be.

01:21:27.740 --> 01:21:29.710 align:middle line:84%
If G doesn't have a
Hamiltonian cycle, pi of G

01:21:29.710 --> 01:21:31.220 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have a
Hamiltonian cycle.

01:21:31.220 --> 01:21:35.860 align:middle line:84%
And that's why I can't
succeed in convincing you

01:21:35.860 --> 01:21:37.700 align:middle line:90%
in answering both questions.

01:21:37.700 --> 01:21:41.200 align:middle line:90%


01:21:41.200 --> 01:21:41.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:21:41.950 --> 01:21:44.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there a
formal way to say what

01:21:44.020 --> 01:21:45.680 align:middle line:90%
it means to be zero knowledge?

01:21:45.680 --> 01:21:46.930 align:middle line:90%
Like, [INAUDIBLE].

01:21:46.930 --> 01:21:48.796 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Yeah, good.

01:21:48.796 --> 01:21:49.610 align:middle line:90%
Good, good, good.

01:21:49.610 --> 01:21:50.330 align:middle line:90%
So the question.

01:21:50.330 --> 01:21:52.663 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, actually, I planned not
to go into the definition,

01:21:52.663 --> 01:21:55.810 align:middle line:84%
but it's so
beautiful, so I will.

01:21:55.810 --> 01:21:56.360 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:21:56.360 --> 01:22:00.200 align:middle line:84%
So the question was,
what is the definition

01:22:00.200 --> 01:22:01.850 align:middle line:90%
actually of zero knowledge?

01:22:01.850 --> 01:22:03.830 align:middle line:84%
And the definition
of zero knowledge

01:22:03.830 --> 01:22:08.220 align:middle line:84%
says that, what does it mean,
you don't learn anything.

01:22:08.220 --> 01:22:11.040 align:middle line:90%
So the definition says that--

01:22:11.040 --> 01:22:16.070 align:middle line:84%
so this is the definition
of zero knowledge.

01:22:16.070 --> 01:22:21.770 align:middle line:84%
What it says is, the
verifier-- actually, it says,

01:22:21.770 --> 01:22:22.530 align:middle line:90%
it's stronger.

01:22:22.530 --> 01:22:26.480 align:middle line:84%
It says, for every verifier,
not just the honest verifier,

01:22:26.480 --> 01:22:27.560 align:middle line:90%
even malicious verifier.

01:22:27.560 --> 01:22:30.140 align:middle line:84%
You can talk about honest
verifier zero knowledge,

01:22:30.140 --> 01:22:33.020 align:middle line:84%
the honest verifier, who
just does what it should do

01:22:33.020 --> 01:22:35.670 align:middle line:84%
and just listens,
doesn't learn anything.

01:22:35.670 --> 01:22:38.160 align:middle line:84%
But actually, there's a
stronger notion of malicious.

01:22:38.160 --> 01:22:44.150 align:middle line:84%
So let's say even for
a malicious verifier,

01:22:44.150 --> 01:22:46.400 align:middle line:90%
you want to say what he learned.

01:22:46.400 --> 01:22:50.600 align:middle line:84%
He could have
simulated on his own.

01:22:50.600 --> 01:22:53.210 align:middle line:84%
So the definition
says, for every--

01:22:53.210 --> 01:22:57.796 align:middle line:84%
so you-- OK, for every verifier,
if you look at the transcript--

01:22:57.796 --> 01:23:01.610 align:middle line:90%


01:23:01.610 --> 01:23:07.740 align:middle line:84%
and for every x
in l, for every x,

01:23:07.740 --> 01:23:10.400 align:middle line:84%
if you look at the transcript
between the honest prover

01:23:10.400 --> 01:23:15.150 align:middle line:84%
and the verifier, the
prover has a witness,

01:23:15.150 --> 01:23:17.810 align:middle line:90%
but the verifier doesn't.

01:23:17.810 --> 01:23:19.910 align:middle line:84%
What he learned from
this transcript or what

01:23:19.910 --> 01:23:21.000 align:middle line:90%
the verifier maybe--

01:23:21.000 --> 01:23:22.530 align:middle line:84%
sorry, let me write
it differently.

01:23:22.530 --> 01:23:29.450 align:middle line:84%
Let me say the view of
v star while interacting

01:23:29.450 --> 01:23:34.880 align:middle line:90%
with p and input x.

01:23:34.880 --> 01:23:42.190 align:middle line:84%
So whatever V star learned, he
could have simulated on his own.

01:23:42.190 --> 01:23:46.850 align:middle line:90%


01:23:46.850 --> 01:23:52.610 align:middle line:84%
So it says there
exists a simulator.

01:23:52.610 --> 01:23:57.855 align:middle line:84%
And this is efficient,
an efficient simulator,

01:23:57.855 --> 01:24:04.610 align:middle line:84%
such that the simulator
efficiently can

01:24:04.610 --> 01:24:09.512 align:middle line:84%
generate this transcript on
its own if he has V star.

01:24:09.512 --> 01:24:10.970 align:middle line:84%
He needs V star
because, of course,

01:24:10.970 --> 01:24:13.280 align:middle line:84%
V star depends what
he sends and so on.

01:24:13.280 --> 01:24:16.610 align:middle line:84%
But he could generate
it on his own.

01:24:16.610 --> 01:24:17.582 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:24:17.582 --> 01:24:19.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can the
stimulator depend

01:24:19.040 --> 01:24:21.010 align:middle line:84%
on V star if we're
then using [INAUDIBLE]?

01:24:21.010 --> 01:24:23.100 align:middle line:84%
So then, is it the same
simulator for everyone?

01:24:23.100 --> 01:24:23.610 align:middle line:90%
Or is it--

01:24:23.610 --> 01:24:25.527 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Oh, my
God, there are so many--

01:24:25.527 --> 01:24:28.890 align:middle line:84%
OK, there's so, so many
notions of zero knowledge.

01:24:28.890 --> 01:24:30.897 align:middle line:90%
So for example, first of all--

01:24:30.897 --> 01:24:31.980 align:middle line:90%
I'll answer your question.

01:24:31.980 --> 01:24:35.150 align:middle line:84%
The question was, is
simulator only gets V star

01:24:35.150 --> 01:24:36.360 align:middle line:90%
as Oracle access?

01:24:36.360 --> 01:24:37.440 align:middle line:90%
Does it get an input?

01:24:37.440 --> 01:24:38.700 align:middle line:90%
Is it universal?

01:24:38.700 --> 01:24:41.810 align:middle line:84%
Is it for there exists a
simulator for every V star,

01:24:41.810 --> 01:24:44.300 align:middle line:84%
or is it for every V star
there is a simulator?

01:24:44.300 --> 01:24:46.140 align:middle line:90%
Moreover, let me continue.

01:24:46.140 --> 01:24:47.730 align:middle line:90%
Is V star all-powerful?

01:24:47.730 --> 01:24:50.950 align:middle line:84%
Is V star supposed to
be PPT or a poly size?

01:24:50.950 --> 01:24:55.850 align:middle line:90%


01:24:55.850 --> 01:24:59.090 align:middle line:84%
And the answer is,
there's many definitions.

01:24:59.090 --> 01:25:04.400 align:middle line:90%
But yeah.

01:25:04.400 --> 01:25:14.810 align:middle line:84%
So originally, all the protocols
we had used the verifier

01:25:14.810 --> 01:25:17.660 align:middle line:90%
as a black box.

01:25:17.660 --> 01:25:22.130 align:middle line:84%
However, in 2001,
I think was it,

01:25:22.130 --> 01:25:27.003 align:middle line:84%
Boaz Barak was the first
to construct non-black box

01:25:27.003 --> 01:25:28.670 align:middle line:84%
zero knowledge, where
the simulator used

01:25:28.670 --> 01:25:30.210 align:middle line:90%
verifier is a non-black box.

01:25:30.210 --> 01:25:33.300 align:middle line:84%
And it was a really
breakthrough result

01:25:33.300 --> 01:25:38.450 align:middle line:84%
because what he managed to get
is a new zero knowledge proof

01:25:38.450 --> 01:25:39.960 align:middle line:90%
that we didn't know of before.

01:25:39.960 --> 01:25:42.560 align:middle line:90%
It was kind of constant round.

01:25:42.560 --> 01:25:45.930 align:middle line:84%
Now you can say, wait, this
is also constant round.

01:25:45.930 --> 01:25:47.870 align:middle line:84%
What do you mean we
didn't know before?

01:25:47.870 --> 01:25:49.450 align:middle line:90%
But this is only soundness half.

01:25:49.450 --> 01:25:52.730 align:middle line:90%


01:25:52.730 --> 01:25:55.980 align:middle line:84%
Turns out, if you want
negligible soundness,

01:25:55.980 --> 01:25:58.860 align:middle line:84%
you need to repeat this
protocol sequentially.

01:25:58.860 --> 01:26:03.600 align:middle line:84%
If you repeat it
in parallel, it's

01:26:03.600 --> 01:26:05.430 align:middle line:90%
not necessarily zero knowledge.

01:26:05.430 --> 01:26:08.530 align:middle line:84%
Actually, yeah, this one
actually is not zero knowledge

01:26:08.530 --> 01:26:09.870 align:middle line:90%
if you repeat it in parallel.

01:26:09.870 --> 01:26:11.850 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk about that.

01:26:11.850 --> 01:26:15.300 align:middle line:84%
And so, using
non-black techniques,

01:26:15.300 --> 01:26:18.600 align:middle line:84%
he managed to show the
first kind of constant round

01:26:18.600 --> 01:26:21.280 align:middle line:84%
zero knowledge-- public
coin zero knowledge proof.

01:26:21.280 --> 01:26:30.140 align:middle line:84%
So there's many, many
variants is the point.

01:26:30.140 --> 01:26:32.760 align:middle line:90%


01:26:32.760 --> 01:26:37.410 align:middle line:84%
But this is a common
variant to think about.

01:26:37.410 --> 01:26:40.660 align:middle line:84%
And also, so this is
kind of poly size,

01:26:40.660 --> 01:26:42.820 align:middle line:90%
or V can have auxiliary input.

01:26:42.820 --> 01:26:45.970 align:middle line:84%
So for every x in [INAUDIBLE]
or V star is poly size.

01:26:45.970 --> 01:26:50.940 align:middle line:84%
So we can have auxiliary
information about the x,

01:26:50.940 --> 01:26:53.200 align:middle line:84%
even if it has auxiliary
information about x, still,

01:26:53.200 --> 01:26:54.580 align:middle line:90%
so V star is--

01:26:54.580 --> 01:26:58.860 align:middle line:84%
may have auxiliary
information about x, still

01:26:58.860 --> 01:27:02.100 align:middle line:90%
the two are indistinguishable.

01:27:02.100 --> 01:27:03.870 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:27:03.870 --> 01:27:10.920 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What if the prover for
the commitment where he created

01:27:10.920 --> 01:27:13.380 align:middle line:84%
matrices that
ensure that there's

01:27:13.380 --> 01:27:16.500 align:middle line:84%
a cycle in that matrix
that commits to that?

01:27:16.500 --> 01:27:19.260 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Wait,
again, what if the prover

01:27:19.260 --> 01:27:21.060 align:middle line:90%
commits to a cycle or no?

01:27:21.060 --> 01:27:23.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: He commits to a cycle,
but the cycle is not necessarily

01:27:23.880 --> 01:27:25.207 align:middle line:90%
with--

01:27:25.207 --> 01:27:26.040 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:27:26.040 --> 01:27:27.780 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: It has
nothing to do with G. Just

01:27:27.780 --> 01:27:28.695 align:middle line:90%
commit to any cycle.

01:27:28.695 --> 01:27:29.010 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:27:29.010 --> 01:27:29.730 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK, good.

01:27:29.730 --> 01:27:30.470 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: He commits to a cycle.

01:27:30.470 --> 01:27:30.730 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: OK.

01:27:30.730 --> 01:27:31.647 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

01:27:31.647 --> 01:27:35.800 align:middle line:84%
And he'll be able to
[INAUDIBLE] it's a 0, right?

01:27:35.800 --> 01:27:37.800 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: Yeah, he'll
be able to-- exactly.

01:27:37.800 --> 01:27:39.175 align:middle line:84%
He'll be able to
answer 0, but he

01:27:39.175 --> 01:27:44.250 align:middle line:84%
won't be able to answer
1 because that cycle must

01:27:44.250 --> 01:27:46.530 align:middle line:90%
touch an edge.

01:27:46.530 --> 01:27:50.850 align:middle line:84%
Because-- sorry, that cycle
must touch a non-edge.

01:27:50.850 --> 01:27:55.960 align:middle line:84%
Because if it only touched
edges, there is no cycle.

01:27:55.960 --> 01:27:58.540 align:middle line:90%
So that cycle, you give your pi.

01:27:58.540 --> 01:28:00.390 align:middle line:84%
Now, the prover can--
the cheating prover

01:28:00.390 --> 01:28:02.710 align:middle line:90%
can give whatever pi he wants.

01:28:02.710 --> 01:28:06.940 align:middle line:84%
But the point is that the
graph, the permuted graph,

01:28:06.940 --> 01:28:09.793 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have a
Hamiltonian cycle,

01:28:09.793 --> 01:28:11.710 align:middle line:84%
because if G doesn't
have a Hamiltonian cycle,

01:28:11.710 --> 01:28:14.370 align:middle line:84%
the permuted G also doesn't
have a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:28:14.370 --> 01:28:17.670 align:middle line:84%
So this graph doesn't have a
Hamiltonian cycle, and that

01:28:17.670 --> 01:28:20.580 align:middle line:84%
means that the
cycle that's here,

01:28:20.580 --> 01:28:22.830 align:middle line:84%
I don't care how you generate,
whatever the cycle was,

01:28:22.830 --> 01:28:25.840 align:middle line:90%
it must touch a non-edge.

01:28:25.840 --> 01:28:27.390 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:28:27.390 --> 01:28:29.490 align:middle line:84%
YAEL T. KALAI: So
the Hamiltonian cycle

01:28:29.490 --> 01:28:31.780 align:middle line:84%
says, there's an
edge between i1, i2.

01:28:31.780 --> 01:28:33.930 align:middle line:90%
There's an edge between i2, i3.

01:28:33.930 --> 01:28:35.860 align:middle line:90%
One of them cannot be edge here.

01:28:35.860 --> 01:28:39.120 align:middle line:84%
It can't be that all of them
are edges here because they're--

01:28:39.120 --> 01:28:42.220 align:middle line:84%
so because it doesn't
have a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:28:42.220 --> 01:28:45.010 align:middle line:84%
So one of them cannot
be an edge here.

01:28:45.010 --> 01:28:48.520 align:middle line:84%
And now, what the verifier
is asking from the prover,

01:28:48.520 --> 01:28:52.270 align:middle line:84%
he looks at all the i1
and i-- all the i and j

01:28:52.270 --> 01:28:53.750 align:middle line:90%
that do not have an edge.

01:28:53.750 --> 01:28:55.277 align:middle line:84%
And he asked them,
open that edge.

01:28:55.277 --> 01:28:56.360 align:middle line:90%
I want to see that it's 0.

01:28:56.360 --> 01:29:01.040 align:middle line:84%
I want to see that I
gave you n squared safes.

01:29:01.040 --> 01:29:03.310 align:middle line:84%
So for every i and
j there is a safe.

01:29:03.310 --> 01:29:06.860 align:middle line:84%
And it should be 1 if there
is an edge, and 0 otherwise--

01:29:06.860 --> 01:29:09.400 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if there is an edge
in the Hamiltonian cycle and 0

01:29:09.400 --> 01:29:10.340 align:middle line:90%
otherwise.

01:29:10.340 --> 01:29:15.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, what the verifier is asking
the prover, for every ij here

01:29:15.970 --> 01:29:17.980 align:middle line:84%
that does not have
an edge, I want

01:29:17.980 --> 01:29:20.450 align:middle line:84%
you to open the safe
corresponding to that edge,

01:29:20.450 --> 01:29:21.980 align:middle line:90%
and I want to see a 0 there.

01:29:21.980 --> 01:29:23.480 align:middle line:84%
If there's a 1
there, I'm not happy.

01:29:23.480 --> 01:29:24.820 align:middle line:90%
I'm going to reject you.

01:29:24.820 --> 01:29:28.510 align:middle line:84%
Because this, there should be
1's only on the Hamiltonian

01:29:28.510 --> 01:29:29.090 align:middle line:90%
cycle.

01:29:29.090 --> 01:29:32.800 align:middle line:84%
And these are the only places
that have an edge in your graph.

01:29:32.800 --> 01:29:37.180 align:middle line:84%
But the problem is, on
one of these ij that

01:29:37.180 --> 01:29:40.930 align:middle line:84%
don't have an edge, you'll see
an edge because the Hamiltonian

01:29:40.930 --> 01:29:43.520 align:middle line:90%
cycle is not all on edges.

01:29:43.520 --> 01:29:45.820 align:middle line:90%
And then I'm going to catch you.

01:29:45.820 --> 01:29:47.155 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:29:47.155 --> 01:29:47.655 align:middle line:90%
Great.

01:29:47.655 --> 01:29:52.540 align:middle line:90%


01:29:52.540 --> 01:29:55.900 align:middle line:90%
Any more questions?

01:29:55.900 --> 01:29:56.630 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:29:56.630 --> 01:29:57.230 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I'm just curious.

01:29:57.230 --> 01:29:59.780 align:middle line:84%
Is there also a notion for
a proof of knowledge about--

01:29:59.780 --> 01:30:00.760 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Yeah.

01:30:00.760 --> 01:30:04.220 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: --just not just
edge, so a slightly stronger

01:30:04.220 --> 01:30:08.520 align:middle line:84%
that requires the prover to
actually know the Hamiltonian

01:30:08.520 --> 01:30:09.020 align:middle line:90%
cycle?

01:30:09.020 --> 01:30:11.320 align:middle line:84%
So here the proof,
would the second part

01:30:11.320 --> 01:30:15.320 align:middle line:84%
say that, that only guarantees
that if it's G is not a--

01:30:15.320 --> 01:30:18.640 align:middle line:84%
doesn't have a Hamiltonian
cycle, that the prover cheat,

01:30:18.640 --> 01:30:21.310 align:middle line:84%
prover cannot compute
this [INAUDIBLE].

01:30:21.310 --> 01:30:25.630 align:middle line:84%
Isn't necessarily a converse,
is it possible for the cheating

01:30:25.630 --> 01:30:28.040 align:middle line:84%
prover to not know
the Hamiltonian cycle,

01:30:28.040 --> 01:30:29.510 align:middle line:90%
but still contains the verifier?

01:30:29.510 --> 01:30:30.320 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Good, no.

01:30:30.320 --> 01:30:30.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Where the G is--

01:30:30.550 --> 01:30:31.150 align:middle line:90%
YAEL T. KALAI: Good.

01:30:31.150 --> 01:30:32.442 align:middle line:90%
Great question, great question.

01:30:32.442 --> 01:30:33.400 align:middle line:90%
So the question was--

01:30:33.400 --> 01:30:36.460 align:middle line:84%
or let me just
answer the question.

01:30:36.460 --> 01:30:41.000 align:middle line:84%
The question was, wait, we
just proved here soundness,

01:30:41.000 --> 01:30:45.340 align:middle line:84%
that if x is a NAND language, on
one of the answers you'll fail.

01:30:45.340 --> 01:30:47.350 align:middle line:84%
But actually,
doesn't this protocol

01:30:47.350 --> 01:30:50.030 align:middle line:84%
give you something
stronger, was the question,

01:30:50.030 --> 01:30:51.200 align:middle line:90%
and the answer is yes.

01:30:51.200 --> 01:30:54.100 align:middle line:84%
So what do we mean by
something stronger?

01:30:54.100 --> 01:30:59.290 align:middle line:84%
Let's say G does have
a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:30:59.290 --> 01:31:03.460 align:middle line:84%
Doesn't this protocol
tell us, not only

01:31:03.460 --> 01:31:05.300 align:middle line:90%
that G has a Hamiltonian cycle.

01:31:05.300 --> 01:31:08.290 align:middle line:84%
If you succeed, let's say you
succeed probability 1 or more

01:31:08.290 --> 01:31:10.060 align:middle line:90%
than half.

01:31:10.060 --> 01:31:13.780 align:middle line:84%
This protocol tells us not only
that G has a Hamiltonian cycle,

01:31:13.780 --> 01:31:18.170 align:middle line:84%
it means that P
must know the cycle.

01:31:18.170 --> 01:31:23.000 align:middle line:84%
You can actually find-- you
can extract the cycle from P.

01:31:23.000 --> 01:31:24.345 align:middle line:90%
And the answer is yes.

01:31:24.345 --> 01:31:25.720 align:middle line:84%
You can actually--
this is what's

01:31:25.720 --> 01:31:27.230 align:middle line:90%
called proof of knowledge.

01:31:27.230 --> 01:31:28.698 align:middle line:90%
It means that P knows.

01:31:28.698 --> 01:31:30.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, you can say, what
does it mean that P knows?

01:31:30.740 --> 01:31:33.490 align:middle line:84%
What are we-- this is a
theory class, mathematics.

01:31:33.490 --> 01:31:35.700 align:middle line:90%
What does know mean?

01:31:35.700 --> 01:31:38.620 align:middle line:84%
So what we mean by
P knows, what we

01:31:38.620 --> 01:31:43.900 align:middle line:84%
mean is that we can
actually efficiently extract

01:31:43.900 --> 01:31:45.650 align:middle line:90%
the cycle from P.

01:31:45.650 --> 01:31:47.430 align:middle line:90%
And how do we extract the cycle?

01:31:47.430 --> 01:31:49.430 align:middle line:90%
It's actually very easy.

01:31:49.430 --> 01:31:50.550 align:middle line:90%
We ask him.

01:31:50.550 --> 01:31:52.110 align:middle line:90%
He committed to something.

01:31:52.110 --> 01:31:53.790 align:middle line:90%
He knows how to open both.

01:31:53.790 --> 01:31:54.900 align:middle line:90%
That's an assumption.

01:31:54.900 --> 01:31:59.320 align:middle line:84%
So first we'll ask him,
open only the cycle.

01:31:59.320 --> 01:32:02.090 align:middle line:90%


01:32:02.090 --> 01:32:08.120 align:middle line:90%
And then give me the pi.

01:32:08.120 --> 01:32:13.050 align:middle line:84%
Once I know the pi and
the cycle, I can undo--

01:32:13.050 --> 01:32:15.050 align:middle line:90%
I know what pi G and I can see--

01:32:15.050 --> 01:32:18.650 align:middle line:84%
I can actually find
the Hamiltonian cycle.

01:32:18.650 --> 01:32:21.837 align:middle line:84%
So it's more than
just a soundness.

01:32:21.837 --> 01:32:22.920 align:middle line:90%
It's a proof of knowledge.

01:32:22.920 --> 01:32:24.200 align:middle line:90%
G must know.

01:32:24.200 --> 01:32:28.250 align:middle line:84%
I can actually use him to
extract the Hamiltonian cycle

01:32:28.250 --> 01:32:28.750 align:middle line:90%
from him.

01:32:28.750 --> 01:32:31.760 align:middle line:90%


01:32:31.760 --> 01:32:34.670 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

01:32:34.670 --> 01:32:37.530 align:middle line:84%
So let's take a
five-minute break.

01:32:37.530 --> 01:32:39.083 align:middle line:84%
And then, after the
break, I'm going

01:32:39.083 --> 01:32:41.750 align:middle line:84%
to show you why this protocol is
not secure in the Random Oracle

01:32:41.750 --> 01:32:42.390 align:middle line:90%
Model.

01:32:42.390 --> 01:32:43.350 align:middle line:90%
But we'll fix it.

01:32:43.350 --> 01:32:43.940 align:middle line:90%
Don't worry.

01:32:43.940 --> 01:32:44.580 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:32:44.580 --> 01:32:46.390 align:middle line:90%
Let's take a break.